window.SSS_CHAPTERS=(window.SSS_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":1,"title":"Chapter 1: No class","paragraphs":["Volume 1: First step","\"Success is not final, failure is not fatal: it is the courage to continue that counts.\" - Winston Churchill","------------------------------------------------------","Rael Blackthorn woke while the sky over the eastern ranges still held the last thin edge of night. The climate regulators in his chambers hummed at their usual low pitch, keeping the air cool and dry. He dressed in the formal academy uniform without ceremony, the dark fabric marked only by the silver thread of the Blackthorn crest.","Outside the tall windows the capital of the Kingdom of Solaria stretched across the surface of Titan in layered districts that rose for kilometers. The planet earned its name through sheer scale. Continents the size of old worlds, oceans that demanded weeks of travel even by the fastest craft, cities that never truly ended. The ducal palace occupied an elevated plateau on the capital’s eastern flank, its walls formed of adaptive alloys and its gardens tended by machines that worked without rest. Rael had lived inside these walls all his life, yet he had never treated them as a place of idle privilege.","He was the son of one of Solaria’s most powerful dukes, the Blackthorn Family. From the earliest years he had refused the path of the ordinary royal brat. While other young nobles wasted their time on status contests and empty displays, Rael studied. At eleven he had already finished the advanced theoretical courses that most students reached only in their final academy years. Once he saw something, he never forgot it. Formulas, combat sequences, historical records of past awakenings—everything stayed sharp in his mind. That gift, combined with the Blackthorn bloodline, had made him the top student of his generation. His older brothers and sisters had all received overpowered classes. His parents stood among the strongest awakeners of the last half-century. Rael expected the same for himself. He had trained for it every day.","The Awakening Ceremony marked the end of childhood on Titan. At sixteen a person was considered an adult. The government and the great houses of Solaria had long since decided there was no point in forcing the young to spend their strongest years buried in classrooms. Education finished early. After the ceremony those who awakened focused entirely on growing their power and preparing for what came next. Those who failed settled into ordinary lives of manual labor or administrative work. Rael, born to a ducal house, had never spent much thought on them.","The Dream Realm waited for every successful awakener. One month after the ceremony the body of a newly empowered person began transferring each night into another place. There they had to survive, gather strength, and eventually find one of the dimensional gates that returned them to Titan. No one knew why the process existed, but even then it happened to every single human. The royal family and the high houses prepared their children from a young age precisely because of it. Bloodline, early exposure to dense mana environments, and rigorous training all improved the odds of a strong result. Rael walked the long corridor toward the private transit bay with that certainty settled firmly in his chest.","He headed for his car—a sleek white flyer with black strips that looked like it belonged in the upper atmosphere rather than on the ground. The driver punched in the destination without a word. University of Solaria.","The campus sat near the heart of the capital, a sprawl of white-stone halls and open plazas big enough to swallow entire districts of lesser cities. The main auditorium already held thousands. Powerful families packed the elevated galleries and the front rows. Ducal banners hung beside the royal crest of Solaria. Representatives from the major houses sat in ordered blocks, a quiet reminder that hierarchy still ruled even in an age of orbital stations and planetary engineering. Students from lesser families and the general population filled the wider seats behind them. The air carried the faint ozone scent of active shielding fields and the low murmur of expectation.","Rael took his place among the candidates of noble blood. One by one the names were called.","Susan Strom stepped onto the central stage and set her hand against the Awakening Stone. Light flared. Stormcaller. Applause rose and settled. Harry Ophid followed and received Serpentmaster. Percy gained Tidecaller. The results continued in a steady rhythm of moderate talent and quiet acceptance. Then the higher houses began to appear.","\"Adrian Gale, come to the stage.\"","Adrian Gale of the rival ducal line walked forward with the easy arrogance Rael had always disliked. The stone pulsed hard. Lightning Swordsman. An S-rank skill. The auditorium erupted. Even Rael brought his hands together once, the sound sharp and brief. Power deserved recognition, whatever its source.","\"Bianca Solaria, Your Highness, please come to the stage.\"","Princess Bianca Solaria rose next. Conversations died across the chamber. She was widely regarded as the most beautiful young woman in the country, and Rael had never been able to look at her without a quiet, private pull of attention. She moved with calm certainty, the daughter of the royal house, and placed her palm against the stone. For a moment nothing happened. Then the light that burst outward was gold and fierce, filling the entire auditorium. Sunforged. An SS-rank class. One in a century. The cheers shook the seating tiers. Rael felt his pulse quicken. If a skill of that magnitude could appear here, then his own result would not disappoint.","\"Rael Blackthorn, please come on stage.\"","His name was called.","Every eye turned. He was the top student of the university, the Blackthorn heir, the one many had already spoken of with certainty. He walked the length of the aisle without rushing, climbed the steps, and stood before the stone. The surface was cool and faintly luminous. He placed his hand flat against it.","Silence stretched.","The light that should have answered did not come. The stone remained dull. A soft mechanical voice spoke from the overhead speakers, clear enough for every powerful family and every ordinary student in the chamber to hear.","\"No class detected.\"","Rael stared at the inert surface. The words hung in the air like a physical weight. Somewhere behind him someone gasped. The first scattered murmurs began to rise.","He pulled his hand back and said, very quietly, \"What the fu—\"","A second attempt was allowed. Protocol. He knew that. Everyone knew that. He set his palm against the stone again, harder this time, as if pressure alone could force an answer. The stone remained silent. The mechanical voice repeated itself, colder now, almost bored.","\"No class detected.\"","---"]},{"number":2,"title":"Chapter 2: Classless","paragraphs":["Rael stood on the stage with his hand still resting against the Awakening Stone. The surface felt cold now, ordinary, as if it had never held any power at all. A single thought kept circling through his mind. Even without talent, even without a strong bloodline, a person could still become an awakener. The records were clear on that. Ordinary citizens had awakened before. But without a class a person was no different from any other human. No mana, no skills, no path into the Dream Realm. Just a body that would live and die on Titan like millions of others who had never touched the stone.","He muttered under his breath, the words barely audible. \"What is happening to me? No class? This can’t be happening. I’m a genius. A prodigy. I don’t have a class?\" He stared at the dull surface. \"The stone is broken. Yes. That’s the reason.\"","He turned toward the examiner. \"The stone is broken. Let me take another test.\"","The examiner’s expression did not change. He had seen this before. \"Mr. Rael, please exit the stage.\"","\"But you see,\" Rael said, his voice rising, \"this can’t be happening. How can I have no skill?\"","It was not the first time the examiner had watched someone break down after the stone refused them. He kept his tone even. \"Sir, the stone is fine. Please exit the stage.\"","Rael still did not move. The examiner hesitated only because of the name Blackthorn. The heir of that house could not be dismissed the same way as an ordinary student. He gave a short nod. \"All right. Place your hand again.\"","Rael pressed his palm flat against the stone in a rush of panic. The same soft mechanical voice answered from the speakers above the stage.","\"No class detected.\"","Something inside him stopped. His mind went blank for a moment, then everything turned hazy, as if the air itself had thickened. The edges of the auditorium blurred. He heard laughter begin in the seats. It started small and spread. Jokes passed from one row to the next. Someone nearby spoke to a child in a clear, carrying voice. \"See? That’s why education doesn’t matter in this world. Only power does.\"","Princess Bianca watched him from the side with one careful glance. Other children of the powerhouses did not bother with subtlety. They pointed. They laughed openly. Rael heard every word, yet none of it fully reached him. His thoughts moved too slowly. He stepped down from the stage and walked back toward his seat. The friends who had sat beside him earlier had already shifted farther away, as if proximity itself carried some kind of sickness. Eve Starborne, the girl who used to stay close and look at him with quiet interest, was pulled back by Matthew Rockwell who was Rael’s bestfriend before. Matthew’s hand rested on her arm. A smirk sat on his face as he guided her a few seats farther down the row.","Rael sat. For the first time in his life his mind was completely empty. No formulas. No remembered lectures. No plans. Just a blank space where certainty used to live. The ceremony continued around him. Names were called. Lights flared. New classes were announced. He registered none of it.","High above, in the gallery reserved for the royal family and the highest houses, Empress Celeste Solaria watched the stage with a faint frown. She was the most powerful woman in the kingdom, and her voice carried easily when she spoke. \"What a poor boy. I feel bad for him.\" She turned her gaze toward Duke Axel Blackthorn, Rael’s father, and the pity in her expression was open. \"I thought that boy would have been one of the pillars of the kingdom.\"","Behind her, Duke Victor Gale gave a short laugh. \"Yeah, Empress. Now he will make the pillars for this kingdom.\"","The other noble representatives joined in. Soft laughter moved along the gallery. Empress Celeste said nothing more. Axel’s face did not change by a single degree. A knight standing at his shoulder slammed the flat of a sword against the floor three times.","Thud Thud Thud...","The signal was clear. The Blackthorn family was leaving. Empress Celeste watched them go and spoke once more, almost to herself. \"Still the stone face as ever, huh.\"","Duke Axel Blackthorn walked out with the family elders and the house knights. No one spoke to him. In the seats below, the reaction was mixed. Many felt a quiet disappointment. The boy’s past had suggested he would become a monster of talent. Others felt something closer to relief. The Blackthorns already held enough monsters. Another one would have shifted the balance further.","The ceremony ran to its end. Only one percent of the candidates failed to awaken. Almost everyone in the university came from a powerful bloodline, so the outcome had never been in real doubt. The last names were called, the last lights flared, and the formal proceedings closed.","Rael’s old group gathered near the front rows. Eve, Matthew, and the others moved toward Adrian Gale. They offered congratulations for the S-rank Lightning Blade. They bowed when Princess Bianca passed. She nodded once and continued on without slowing she once more glanced at Rael then left the auditorium. Then the group began to talk among themselves about the Dream Realm, about meeting inside it, about working together once the night transfers began. They knew how deeply Rael hated Adrian. That knowledge only made the conversation louder. They glanced toward the seat where Rael still sat with empty eyes and dead posture. Laughter rose again. Someone called him trash. The brightest star, the hope of the Solaria kingdom, reduced to trash. They laughed longer, then left the auditorium in a loose group, still talking. After them many students left some smiling and some with tears in there eyes because of having a weak class.","Rael watched them go. A single clear thought cut through the haze. He would have killed for any of the weak classes those others were crying about. Even the lowest rank would have been enough. Anything except this, except being classless.","When the last students and the last officials had gone, the vast chamber fell quiet. Only then did Rael cry. The tears came without sound at first, then with the full weight of everything he had carried. Every expectation he had built for his life poured out of him. The childhood dream of becoming the most powerful awakener on Titan, of standing as a hero, ended in that empty auditorium. He would never enter the Dream Realm. He would never awaken. The path was closed.","He looked up toward the high ceiling, toward whatever might be listening beyond the metal and stone. His voice broke when he shouted.","\"Why? Why me? I have given everything I had to become powerful, and you made those slacking bastards have power but not me. Why? You bastard god, why?\"","Someone sat in the upper stands, watching. A figure stepped out of the shadows beside that person and spoke in a low voice. \"My lord, we should leave now.\" The seated figure looked at Rael for one more moment, then rose and walked away without answering.","When the tears finally stopped, Rael stood and walked out of the auditorium. The private car that should have waited for him was gone. He started walking toward home under the layered lights of the capital, the long roads of Titan stretching ahead of him in silence.","---"]},{"number":3,"title":"Chapter 3: Expelled","paragraphs":["Rael walked the long roads back toward the Blackthorn mansion with his thoughts finally settling into something colder and clearer. The breakdown in the empty auditorium had burned itself out. What remained was a practical question that refused to leave him alone. What could he do now?","He could not become an awakener. The stone had made that final. Without a class he would never pass through the gates of the Dream Realm. He could not work as a trainer either. Training required power of one’s own, or at least the ability to sense and guide mana, he can work on research and development but even that pays less. Every option he can either do is less pay and what he can’t do has more money. His memory was still sharp. That much had not changed. He could recall every statute, every case summary, every procedural detail he had ever studied. Legal work for the family might have been possible in another life. But the Blackthorns already employed people with actual classes suited to those tasks. A classless genius would only be an inconvenience.","He kept walking. The decision formed slowly. He would live a meaningful life in the background. His siblings would take the leading roles. He could still advise them, still help the family from the shadows where no one expected power. It was not the future he had planned, but it was something. He would not simply disappear.","The mansion gates came into view under the layered lights of the capital. Rael slowed. Everything he owned sat in neat piles along the outer wall. Medals and awards from every academic year. Research books he had annotated in the margins. Clothes, personal devices, the small collection of study tools he had used since childhood. All of it dumped on the pavement like discarded trash. The heavy gates remained shut.","\"What are my things doing outside?\"","He stepped closer and called to the nearest guard. \"Hey. Open the door.\"","The man did not move from his post. \"Sorry, young master. His Highness ordered the gates to remain closed for the whole day.\"","Rael stared. \"What? Do you know who I am? Open it at once.\"","\"I’m sorry, young master. I can’t.\"","Anger rose fast. \"Are you joking with me? Open the gates, bastard.\"","A second guard approached, one hand rubbing at his ear as if the volume alone had offended him. He looked straight into Rael’s eyes. \"Who is shouting so much at the evening?\" The man’s voice carried open contempt. \"Hey, you classless trash. Show some respect for Master-level peoples.\"","Rael felt the words land like a physical blow. A guard. Speaking to him like that. \"What?\"","The second guard jerked his chin toward the piles of belongings. \"Don’t you see? All your things have been thrown out. Pick them up and leave.\"","Understanding arrived in a single cold rush. He had been kicked out. One day earlier he had been the bright star of the family. Now the gates of his own home stayed closed against him. The shift was so complete it felt almost unreal.","\"Bring someone important,\" Rael said. His voice stayed level only through force of will. \"Bring your superior. I will talk with him.\"","The guard’s mouth twisted. \"Get lost, trash. Don’t waste our time. You are no longer needed here.\"","Footsteps sounded behind the second man. Leon Greythrone, Rael’s step-uncle and the head of mansion security, stepped into view. He moved without hurry, reached out, and smacked the back of the guard’s head hard enough to make the man stagger. \"Show some respect for the son of his highness of the house.\"","The guard dropped to his knees at once. \"I’m sorry, sir.\"","Leon turned to Rael. His expression held no warmth and took out a paper and started reading it. \"Rael his Highness has expelled you from the family. You will no longer be called Blackthorn. Effective immediately, leave the capital.\"","Rael stood frozen. The words registered, yet they refused to settle into sense. No longer a Blackthorn. Classless. The two facts pressed together until there was no room left for anything else.","Leon’s mouth curved into a small, satisfied smirk. \"His Highness has transferred ten million credits to your account as compensation. He has also demoted your rank to ordinary citizen. Now please exit the capital city.\"","He said with seriousness. \"An ordinary human cannot walk the streets of the capital.\"","He turned away without waiting for a reply. The kneeling guard remained on the ground, smiling now that his superior’s back was turned. Rael understood in that moment. The disrespect, the piles of belongings left outside, the open mockery—none of it had been accident. Leon had arranged the performance carefully he can’t directly trash talk with the heir of the family so he used the guard for that purpose.","Rael understood how pathetic he was behaving when he first saw his things outside he already knew what happened but still wanted to talk with someone from the family. The knowledge settled over him like a weight. He had stood at the pinnacle only hours earlier. Everyone had watched him with expectation. Now he was abandoned, clinging to the smallest remaining scraps of dignity while the people who once bowed to him treated him as refuse. The feeling was raw and humiliating and absolute.","He moved to the nearest pile and began sorting through it. One bag. That was all he would take. Clothes enough for a few days. His smartphone. A handful of personal jewelry pieces he could sell later if the credits ran thin. Ten million would not last forever in a world built for the powerful. Everything else—the medals, the annotated books, the careful records of a life that no longer existed—he left where it lay. He was adapting already. The sadness remained, heavy and real, but he was not a quitter. He would find another way to live.","Rael ordered a taxi through the public network. When the vehicle arrived he gave the destination without hesitation. Bloomingdale. The outskirts city beyond the capital’s formal boundaries. He climbed inside and the doors sealed. The cab pulled away from the curb.","High above, behind a reinforced window of the mansion, his mother watched the street. She was so powerful that she can hear every word that had been spoken at the gate. She placed one hand against the glass as the taxi’s lights moved down the long avenue. A single tear tracked down her cheek.","\"Good luck, my sweet boy,\" she whispered.","Then she turned away and stopped watching.","---"]},{"number":4,"title":"Chapter 4: Status","paragraphs":["\"It is never too late to be what you might have been.\" — George Eliot","One month later","Rael sat on the edge of the narrow bed in his small apartment on the outskirts of Bloomingdale. The room was quiet except for the low hum of the cheap climate unit that struggled against the evening heat. A month had passed since the gates of the Blackthorn mansion closed behind him, and the days had blurred together until they felt like a single long stretch of gray. His body had simply moved through the motions. Buy the apartment. Order the basic furniture. Set up the television. Stock the small kitchen with whatever required the least effort.","He had paid ten thousand credits for the place. More than nine million remained in the account his father had transferred as compensation. The number sat there like an accusation. He did not want to touch it. Every time he considered spending more than the absolute minimum, the memory of Leon’s smirk returned, and the money felt dirty. Yet ordinary work refused him. Interview after interview ended the same way. The moment his background appeared on the screen—former top student of the capital university, former Blackthorn—the expressions across the table changed. No one wanted a classless heir who had been publicly discarded. The rejections piled up until he stopped applying.","Most nights he ate instant noodles because he had never learned to cook properly. The packets stacked in the cupboard were the same brand he had once ignored in the palace kitchens. Now they were dinner. Friends had vanished. His siblings had never called. The silence confirmed what the stone had already declared: he was of no use.","Tonight was different. If he had awakened, this would have been the night his body transferred into the Dream Realm. Classless people stayed behind. The Dream Realm was a separate dimension from Titan, so vast that no one had ever mapped its edges. Some claimed it had no limit at all. Monsters held more than ninety-five percent of the land. Dungeons dotted the safer regions and offered high rewards to those strong enough to clear them. Titan itself was home to more than humans. Other species lived across its continents, some stronger in raw power, yet humans remained among the smartest. Knowledge, however, meant little when a person could not even leave the city walls. Outside the guarded districts the climate turned harsh and the roads unpatrolled. Only awakeners traveled freely between cities. Both Titan and the Dream Realm were so large that the thought of crossing them still gave Rael a faint shiver of longing. He had once planned to see every culture, every landscape. That plan had died with his class.","He lay down at eleven and closed his eyes, letting the familiar fantasy of the Dream Realm carry him under.","The text appeared in front of him while he still believed he was dreaming.","[Host consciousness detected.]","[Welcome, Rael.]","[Welcome to the Dream Realm.]","[A world where strength determines destiny. A world where the weak are prey and the strong become legends. Your journey begins now. May you survive long enough to discover the truth.]","He had read those words a thousand times on public networks when newly awakened people posted their first screenshots. Seeing them float in the dark felt unreal. He opened his eyes.","Grass pressed against his cheek. Tall trees rose on every side, their canopies blocking most of the strange sky. His mind stayed hazy for several seconds. Other figures his own age lay scattered across the clearing, stirring slowly. An insect landed on his ankle and bit hard. The sharp sting cut through the fog. Red welled on the skin. This was real.","Joy hit him so suddenly that for a moment he wanted to slam his forehead into the ground just to prove the sensation belonged to a living body. He was inside the Dream Realm. The impossible had happened. Confusion followed at once. He had no class. The stone had said so twice. Why was he here?","Around him the others sat up, rubbing their eyes, staring at the trees with the same mixture of awe and fear. A woman descended from the open sky on golden wings. White hair moved around her shoulders. She looked like an angel from the old stories, yet her face was human, practical, used to this work. She was a guide. Rael scanned the clearing quickly. No one he recognized from the university or the capital families had appeared near him. Whatever force had brought him here had dropped him among strangers.","When the last of the new arrivals stood, the winged woman spoke. Her voice carried easily across the grass.","\"Newly awakened, welcome to the Dream Realm. I am Miranda, your guide. First I would like you to check your status on the system. Say the word ’Status’ and the screen will appear. It will show your abilities and your power level.\"","Everyone already knew the command. The instruction was tradition more than necessity, part of the official welcome. Rael still obeyed. The word left his mouth almost without sound.","\"Status.\"","The window opened in front of him.","╔═════════════════════╗","SYSTEM","╚═════════════════════╝","[System initiating...]","[Scanning host...]","[Identity confirmed.]","[Name: Rael]","[Race: Human]","[Class detected.]","[Unique Class detected.]","[Class: Copy]","[Initializing Status Window...]","━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━","STATUS","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Level: 1","Souls: 0","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Strength: 10 (+10)","Agility: 10 (+10)","Mana: 10 (+10)","Vitality: 10 (+10)","Skills: None","━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━","[Unique Class: Copy]","F-Rank Copy","Description:","A class that allows the user to temporarily copy and wield the abilities associated with another person’s class. The copied class functions as if it were the user’s own, allowing the user to access its basic class abilities and powers.","A copied class remains active for one month before disappearing. Once the duration expires, the copied class is automatically removed unless it has been copied again.","Number of Classes: 0/3","[Class acquisition function unlocked.]","[Class slots detected: 3]","[Maximum simultaneous copied classes: 3]","[Further evolution of the Copy class may increase its capabilities.]","[System initialization complete.]","Current Copies: None","[Welcome, Rael.]","He read every line twice. The stone at the university could not detect anything below E-Rank. An F-Rank class was almost unheard of, something the official records treated as theoretical. Yet the window was clear. He had a class. Unique. Copy. Three empty slots waiting. A bloodline that doubled every base stat. He was not classless.","The knowledge settled slowly, then all at once. Tears came without warning. He turned his face toward the grass so the others would not see. The crying was quiet and complete. For a month he had lived as discarded weight, useful to no one, locked inside city walls while the rest of his generation stepped into legend. Now the path existed again. He was not strong. The rank was the lowest possible. But he was no longer useless. He could walk the roads of the Dream Realm. He could chase the dream he had carried since childhood.","Miranda’s voice continued in the background, explaining the first rules of survival, the nearest safe zones, the way the night transfers would work from this point forward. Rael barely heard her. He stayed on the ground a little longer, letting the tears finish their work, then pushed himself upright and wiped his face with the back of one hand. The trees of the Dream Realm stood around him, vast and unknown. Somewhere beyond them the dungeons waited, and the monsters, and the truths the system had promised he might live long enough to find.","He was still here. That was enough for the first night.","---"]},{"number":5,"title":"Chapter 5: Flamecaller","paragraphs":["Rael’s eyes moved past the class window and settled on the bloodline entry. Absolute Sovereign. The name was familiar. Every Blackthorn carried a version of it. The family bloodline acted as a multiplier for attributes and soul growth, one of the main reasons the house stood among the strongest in Solaria. Yet the number beside it stopped him cold. One hundred percent. His father’s had been the highest recorded in recent generations at thirty percent. A full doubling of every form of growth was something no one had ever spoken of, even in private family records.","The description unfolded when he focused on it.","[Bloodline: Absolute Sovereign]","A supreme bloodline that grants its bearer absolute growth potential. The power of the Absolute Sovereign strengthens every aspect of the host’s development, allowing Rael to grow far beyond ordinary beings.","Effects:","- +100% Experience Gain","- +100% Soul Acquisition","- +100% Attribute Growth","- +100% Class Growth","- +100% Skill Growth","The effects of the bloodline apply to all forms of legitimate growth obtained by the host.","He stared at the list for a long moment. Then his gaze shifted to the Soul option. The window expanded.","[Soul]","Souls are the essence of living beings and a fundamental source of power within the Dream Realm. Whenever Rael kills a living creature, a portion of its soul is absorbed by the system and converted into Soul Energy.","The number of souls obtained depends on the strength and quality of the defeated creature.","[Absolute Sovereign Effect: Soul Acquisition +100%]","[Usage: Souls can be used to upgrade class and skills.]","Rael was shocked he could get double the amount of souls compared to other. It was like a cheat skill. He can get stronger twice as fast from the others.","Miranda’s voice cut across the clearing before he could dig deeper it was as if she wanted to get this over with as fast as possible. \"You have to escape the beginner stage of the Dream Realm and reach the gate. The monsters here range from level one to ten. All ordinary-rank. That is all for now.\" She rose on her golden wings and vanished into the clouds as quickly as she had arrived.","The daze that had held the group broke. Smiles spread. Voices rose. Excitement was natural. Every person standing in the grass had just received a chance to rewrite their life. Power waited beyond the trees if they survived long enough to claim it. For Rael the feeling ran deeper. Only hours earlier he had not been certain he wanted to keep living. Now the dream he had carried since childhood stood open in front of him. He had a class.","People began clustering into familiar groups. The transfer worked by region. Everyone who had been in the same area of Titan before the night shift arrived together. Most of them already knew one another from the same universities or training halls. Rael recognized none of the faces. That made joining a party harder.","He watched the clearing until he noticed four figures standing a little apart from the larger clusters. Two boys, two girls. They looked less certain than the rest, the kind of students who had never stood at the center of attention back home. Rael decided they were his best chance. He could not escape the beginner stage alone. The old focus returned, steady and cold. Sulking was finished. He would live. He would become powerful again.","He walked toward them. The boy with black hair turned first.","\"You need something?\"","\"Hey,\" Rael said. \"Can I join your party? I don’t know many people here.\"","Rael has a handsome face with a sharp jawline and fit build. His skin was pale white with dark black hairs and black eyes.","Getting infatuated by his looks one of the girls, Suzy, looked him over and smiled. \"Why don’t we let him join?\"","The black-haired boy was Nathan. Beside him stood Alex with brown hair. The second girl, Lily, kept a serious expression and said nothing yet. Nathan studied Rael for a moment.","\"Why us?\"","\"You looked more approachable. You know you guys looks like nice people.\"","Nathan was a easy going guy he could see the honesty when he sees a person. Nathan’s posture eased a fraction. He held out his hand. Rael shook it.","\"All right,\" Nathan said. \"But can you tell us your class?\"","A soft system prompt appeared at the edge of Rael’s vision.","[Class detected]","[Class: Firecaller (D-Rank)]","[Do you want to copy?]","[Y/N]","He selected yes without hesitation. The new information settled into place. He met Nathan’s eyes.","\"I’m a fire mage.\"","Nathan’s eyebrows rose. \"Wow. What a surprise. I’m a Firecaller.\" He gestured to the others. \"Alex is a spearman. Lily’s an archer. Suzy’s a priestess.\"","Rael nodded to each of them in turn. \"Hello. Hope I can be of some help.\"","Suzy stepped a little closer, still smiling. \"Come on. You being here is help enough.\"","Rael gave a short laugh. It was the first time he had seen a priestess act so openly toward a man. Most of them kept careful distance. He filed the detail away and opened the skill list that had come with the copied class.","[Class: Flamecaller ]","[A mage-type class that allows the user to manipulate and command flames. Flamecallers can summon fire, shape it into various forms, and gradually increase the intensity of their flames as their mastery grows.]","[Skills]","[Fireball] (Novice -> Apprentice)","[Summons a concentrated ball of fire and launches it toward the target. The fireball explodes on impact, dealing Fire Damage to the target and nearby enemies. Its power increases with the user’s Mana and mastery of fire.]","[Ignite] (Apprentice -> Expert)","[Ignites the target with a sudden burst of flames, causing Burning. The flames continue dealing damage for a short duration. Stronger targets can resist or reduce the effect, while repeated applications can intensify the burning.]","Both skills were solid for the beginner stage. He wanted to test them, but not here in front of the group. He would wait until they met the first monsters.","Nathan looked at Rael’s empty hands. \"So where’s your wand?\"","Everyone else carried equipment in storage rings—the only reliable way to bring objects from Titan into the Dream Realm. Rael felt the sudden weight of how unprepared he was. That morning he had not even known he would transfer. He needed an answer that would not raise questions.","\"Oh,\" he said. \"I forgot to bring the ring.\"","---"]},{"number":6,"title":"Chapter 6: First Battle","paragraphs":["Rael kept his voice even. \"Oh. I forgot my ring.\"","Nathan stopped walking and stared at him. \"You forgot your ring? How is that even possible? It’s the most important thing during the first awakening. Everyone knows that. Huhhh.\"","Alex shifted his weight, spear resting against his shoulder. \"Seriously? No storage ring at all?\"","Suzy glanced between them, concern flickering across her face. \"Did something happen before you transferred? You look like you came straight from... well, nowhere ready.\"","Rael met their eyes one by one. \"I have higher base stats than most. I can fight without a weapon for now. Close range if I have to.\"","Nathan rubbed the back of his neck, still frowning. \"Oh. We can do that, I guess. Just... stay close until we find something better.\"","Lila said nothing. She watched Rael with quiet attention. She was bright enough to catch the hesitation in his answer, the way his gaze had slid aside for half a second. The story about forgetting the ring felt thin. She decided, without speaking, that caution around the newcomer would be wise. Handsome face or not, something about him did not line up.","They moved into the forest together. The trees grew denser, the light filtering down in broken shafts. The goal was simple: find the gate that marked the end of the beginner stage, and level up along the way. Everyone knew the first transfer offered the safest window for growth. The monsters here stayed within a predictable range if you were careful. Rael had read every public article and academy text on the tutorial stage. Horned rabbits. Goblins. Dire rats. Wolves. Giant spiders. Venom snakes. Those names appeared in nearly every guide. Yet every experienced awakener repeated the same warning: never trust the sources completely. The Dream Realm followed its own rules. Logic that worked on Titan often failed here. Rael walked ready for whatever the forest decided to show them.","They had not gone far when the trees opened into a small clearing. Four goblins stood in a rough circle, short blades in their hands. In the center lay a girl who could not have been older than they were. Her academy uniform was torn open. One of the goblins had already begun peeling the remaining armor away from her body. The others watched with flat, hungry eyes.","The sight hit the party like a physical blow. Suzy turned and vomited into the grass. Nathan followed, then Alex, then Lila. The sound of retching filled the quiet space between the trees. Rael alone remained still. He looked at the dead girl and felt no surge of grief or horror. Only a cold, clear thought: she had died because she was incompetent. Traveling alone on the first transfer was the choice of a fool. If he ever became like her—isolated, unprepared, finished before the real climb began—he would rather not live at all. The goblins disgusted him for a different reason. They killed, then treated the body as something to ravage and later eat. No basic decency. Creatures that existed only to take.","He kept his voice low. \"Guys. Are you ready?\"","They wiped their mouths, faces pale, and nodded. Rael bent and picked up a heavy stone from the ground. His plan was simple: create an opening, take one of the goblin blades, and fight from there. Lila climbed a nearby tree with quick, quiet movements, settling onto a branch that gave her a clear line of sight. Suzy stayed at the rear, hands already glowing with the first threads of healing light. Nathan moved to a safer distance where he could cast without drawing immediate attention. Alex and Rael slipped forward through the undergrowth.","Alex struck first. He came from behind the nearest goblin and drove the spear straight through the back of its skull. The creature dropped without a sound. At the same moment Lila’s arrow took a second goblin through the eye. It staggered, howling, one hand clapped over the ruined socket. Rael stepped in and smashed the stone against its temple with everything he had. Bone gave way. The goblin collapsed. He wrenched the short blade from its slack fingers and turned on the third. The steel slid between ribs before the creature could raise its own weapon.","The last goblin spun toward Nathan, mouth open in a snarl. Nathan’s fireball struck it square in the chest. Flames licked across green skin and scorched the crude leather it wore, but the creature kept coming. Alex lunged and finished it, driving the spear up through its belly until the point exited near the spine.","Silence returned, broken only by heavy breathing. Four goblin bodies lay still. The girl in the center of the clearing remained exactly as they had found her.","Rael wiped the blade clean on the grass. He looked at Nathan. \"Can I borrow the wand for a minute?\"","Nathan hesitated, questions clear on his face, then handed it over without speaking. Rael pointed the wand at the girl’s body. The word left his mouth steady.","\"Fireball.\"","The flame that bloomed was larger and hotter than anything Nathan had produced earlier. Rael’s bloodline had already begun its quiet work. The D-rank skill burned with a force closer to something higher. Fire consumed the body completely, reducing everything to ash and blackened soil. No one spoke while it burned.","Notifications appeared in Rael’s vision, crisp and sequential.","[Level up]","[Level up]","[Soul +10]","[Soul +10]","He had risen two full levels in the single fight. The Absolute—no, the Abyssal Sovereign bloodline had doubled every reward. Twenty souls instead of ten. He called for his status.","STATUS","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank) (Upgrade)","Copied Class: Flamecaller (D-Rank)","Level: 1 → 3","Souls: 0 → 20","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Strength: 24","Agility: 24","Mana: 24","Vitality: 24","Additional Stats: 4","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert)","Two attribute points per level, plus two additional points he could assign later. The numbers settled into place. Around him the others still looked shaken. Lila climbed down from the tree without a word, her earlier suspicion now mixed with something harder to read. Suzy kept her eyes away from the ash. Nathan took the wand back slowly.","Rael closed the status window. The beginner stage had shown its first real face. He intended to meet the rest of it the same way—ready, cold when he needed to be, and moving forward.","---"]},{"number":7,"title":"Chapter 7: Frost Dire Wolf","paragraphs":["The ash from the girl’s body still hung in the air when they left the clearing. No one spoke. It had been their first real kill, and the weight of it sat heavy on all of them. Suzy kept her arms wrapped tightly around herself, shoulders hunched. Alex wiped the spear point on the grass over and over even though the blood was already gone. Lila walked a few steps behind, her eyes never still, flicking between the trees and the back of Rael’s head. Nathan stayed closest to Rael, the wand tucked into his belt again.","Rael looked down at his own hands. This was the first time he had used a skill. He had muttered the name and fire had answered, but he still did not understand how it worked. The Copy class let him borrow the ability. It did not give him the deep knowledge that came with owning a class. Understanding a skill was the real path to mastery. For now he could only use what he had taken. Maybe later, if the class evolved, that would change.","A quiet thought surfaced. If he did not need to understand the skill the way a true Flamecaller did, maybe he did not need a wand either. He waited until they were far enough from the clearing, then raised an empty hand. He focused on the same feeling he had felt when the wand was in his grip and whispered the word.","\"Fireball.\"","A sphere of flame bloomed above his palm. It was smaller than the one that had burned the girl’s body, but it was real and solid and hot. It floated there with no tool to shape it. The system accepted the command without protest. He closed his fist and the fire vanished. A thin, sharp relief moved through him. He would not have to ask for the wand again.","Nathan had seen the brief light. \"You can cast without a wand?\"","\"Yeah,\" Rael said. \"I just thought of it now. Must be the class.\"","Nathan gave a short, uneasy laugh. \"Must be convenient, huh?\"","\"Yeah. To be honest.\" Rael managed a small smile.","They pushed deeper into the forest. The trees drew closer together and the light turned thin and green. Rael kept the copied skills ready at the edge of his mind. Every step felt heavier than it should have. He was level three. The number still surprised him when he let himself think about it.","After a while they stopped to rest and catch their breath. Rael realized again how unprepared he was. The others pulled food from their storage rings. Nathan noticed him watching and held out half a sandwich without a word. Rael took it.","\"Thank you.\"","The attack came while they were still eating.","Low growls rolled out of the undergrowth on three sides at once. Five wolves broke from the shadows, lean and gray, eyes catching what little light reached the forest floor. They moved like they already owned the clearing. The largest of them, half a head taller than the rest, led from the front.","Alex shouted and planted his feet, spear forward. Lila’s bow was already drawn. Suzy’s hands lit with soft white light as she stepped back. Nathan raised his wand. Rael simply lifted both hands.","The first wolf lunged at Alex. The spear caught it in the shoulder, but the animal’s weight still drove him backward. Rael did not wait. He shaped a Fireball and sent it forward. He aimed for the head. The sphere hit the chest instead. The explosion of flame and the sharp smell of burned fur filled the air. The wolf screamed and rolled, trying to put the fire out. Rael followed at once with Ignite. Fresh flames burst across its back and the animal went down thrashing.","Lila’s arrows took a third wolf in the flank and neck. Nathan’s fireball struck the fourth but only scorched its side. The creature kept coming until Alex, free of the first wolf, drove the spear through its ribs. Suzy’s healing light washed over a shallow cut on Alex’s arm before the blood could run far.","The lead wolf circled, looking for an opening. Rael met its eyes and felt nothing but cold focus. He had been discarded, laughed at, stripped of his name. These animals were only another obstacle between him and the gate. He raised his hands again.","Two Fireballs left his palms in quick succession. The first forced the lead wolf to leap aside. The second caught it in mid-air and slammed it into a tree trunk with a heavy crack. Ignite followed. The flames clung to its fur. The beast howled and rolled across the ground until Alex finished it with a clean thrust through the throat.","For a moment the forest was quiet except for heavy breathing and the soft crackle of dying fire. Four wolves lay still. The fifth had fled into the trees with an arrow in its haunch. Rael stood with his hands still raised, the last traces of heat fading from his fingers. He had done most of the killing. The copied skills answered him cleanly, stronger than they should have been for a D-rank class. The bloodline was already showing its value.","A soft chime sounded in his mind.","[Level up]","Nathan wiped sweat from his forehead and managed a shaky grin. \"That was... you were fast.\"","Rael only nodded. He opened his status.","[STATUS]","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Copied Class: Flamecaller (D-Rank)","Level: 3 → 4 (86%)","Souls: 20 → 40","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Strength: 26","Agility: 26","Mana: 26","Vitality: 26","Additional Stats: 6","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert)","Experience and souls had flowed in, doubled by the bloodline. He was already most of the way to the next level.","The party gathered closer, still watching the trees. Lila’s expression stayed careful whenever her eyes passed over Rael. Suzy offered water from a small flask. Alex leaned on his spear and tried to laugh the fear away.","They never saw the larger wolf until it was already moving.","It came from directly behind Nathan, silent and far bigger than the others. Its shoulders stood higher than a man’s chest. Dark fur covered thick, corded muscle that shifted with every breath. Frost clung to its muzzle and the tips of its ears even though the air was not cold. The eyes that locked onto Nathan’s back held a cold, intelligent malice that did not belong in any beginner stage. The pressure that rolled off its body made the air itself feel heavier. Everyone froze. They knew, without speaking, that if they moved a muscle they would die.","Rael’s gaze caught the system tag that flickered above its head for less than a second.","[Frost Dire Wolf]","[Level: 50]","[Rank: Elite]","No one had time to shout a warning.","The wolf closed the last three meters in a single bound. Its jaws opened wide and closed around Nathan’s head with a wet, final crunch. Bone and flesh gave way in the same instant. Nathan’s body dropped straight down. The wand slipped from nerveless fingers. Blood sprayed across the leaves and across Rael’s boots.","For a heartbeat the entire party simply stared.","Suzy’s scream tore the silence first. She stumbled backward, hands pressed to her mouth, healing light flickering uselessly around her fingers. Alex froze with the spear half-raised, face drained of every color. Lila’s bowstring slipped from her fingers; the arrow fell into the grass. Rael felt his mind go blank for a single second, then fill with pure, sharp disbelief.","A level-fifty creature could not exist here. Every guide, every lecture, every survivor account agreed on the same hard limit. The beginner stage held nothing above level ten. The stone at the university, the system itself, the entire structure of the first transfer—all of it said this was impossible.","The massive wolf lifted its head. Blood dripped from its muzzle in slow, heavy drops. Frost spread outward from where its paws touched the ground. It looked at the remaining four humans with calm, almost curious eyes, as if deciding which one to take next. The air around it felt thicker, charged with a pressure that pressed against Rael’s lungs and made every breath feel too shallow. His copied Flamecaller skills still sat ready in his mind, but they suddenly felt small, almost childish, against whatever this thing was.","Alex finally found his voice, raw and breaking. \"That... that can’t be here. It can’t.\"","Lila whispered the same words under her breath, over and over. Suzy had dropped to her knees, staring at the headless body that had been Nathan only seconds before. The blood was still spreading, dark and irreversible, soaking into the forest floor.","Rael forced himself to move. He stepped sideways, putting himself between the wolf and the others. Fireball formed above his right palm, larger and brighter than any he had cast so far. The bloodline pushed everything harder, but even that boost felt thin against the creature watching him.","The Frost Dire Wolf did not attack immediately. It simply stood over Nathan’s body, breathing slowly, eyes never leaving Rael’s face. In that stretched silence every member of the party understood the same terrible truth at once.","Something had broken inside the beginner stage.","And it had just tasted blood.","---"]},{"number":8,"title":"Chapter 8: Run","paragraphs":["Instead of hurling a fireball at the frost wolf, Rael slammed the fireball into the ground. Dried leaves and brittle twigs covered the forest floor in thick layers. The impact sent a burst of smoke rolling upward, thick and choking. The wolf’s claw swept through the haze an instant later, missing him by a hand’s breadth. Rael snatched the wand up again and shouted, \"Split... Split up! Run!\"","They all got out of daze and started running they scattered. Alex and Suzy veered one way. Rael and Lila the other. As some cleared the monster saw the pair running towards the upper slope and behind them the frost wolf lunged after the first pair, its heavy body crashing through undergrowth. Rael did not look back. He threw himself down the slope with everything he had left, lungs burning, legs driving hard. If he turned, he knew he would see the creature. That single glance might be enough to freeze him in place.","Lila ran beside him, close enough that he could hear her ragged breathing. The ground changed under their feet without warning. Grass that had felt firm a moment earlier turned slick and treacherous, as if the whole hillside had been coated in ice. Lila’s foot shot out from under her. She pitched forward with a short, startled cry.","Rael lunged sideways and caught her around the waist. He knew her vitality was nowhere near his. A fall like that would break her bones. They slid together, his boots skidding, her weight dragging him off balance. A tree trunk rose out of the blur. His head struck it hard. Pain exploded behind his eyes. Consciousness flickered like a dying lamp. He locked his arms around Lila and held on as they tumbled.","Darkness closed in, then cracked open again. Lila’s face swam above him, pale and close. Her voice came from a long way off.","\"Rael... Rael... wake up.\"","The words slipped away. Darkness took him once more.","When his eyes opened the next time, the world was moving. Stone scraped against his back. Someone was pulling him by the wrist, dragging him across rough ground. Distant screams reached him—high, terrified, cut short. He tried to lift his head. The effort cost him another stretch of black nothing.","He woke in a narrow space that barely deserved the name of cave. More like a hollow scraped into the rock, low ceiling, damp walls. Lila crouched at the entrance, shoving bushes and broken branches across the opening until only thin slits of light remained. Rael pushed himself up on one elbow. Pain slammed through his skull so hard he nearly blacked out again.","Lila was at his side in two steps. She pressed him gently back down.","\"Don’t. Just stay still. We’re safe for now.\"","Her voice still carried that distant echo, as if his ears had not finished waking. She pulled a water bottle from her storage ring and tipped it carefully against his lips. He drank, spilled half of it down his chin, then coughed. Blood came with the cough, bright against the dirt floor. His hand went to his head and found a sticky, cool paste already spread across the wound.","\"Medicine,\" Lila said quietly. \"It’s the best I have.\"","Rael let his head fall back. The thought drifted through the fog: he should have copied Suzy’s class when he had the chance. A priestess skill might have fixed this. Instead he lay there with Lila’s presence a steady warmth at his side and let sleep take him again, the only safety he could feel.","Hours—or days—later he opened his eyes to find her asleep in the far corner, knees drawn up, head resting against the stone. The pain in his skull had eased enough that he could sit without coughing blood. The memory of the frost wolf returned in a rush.","A level 50 monster in the first awakening realm. Impossible. It could not happen. It simply could not.","The small sound of his movement woke her. Lila’s eyes opened, still heavy with sleep. For a second she stared at him as if she had forgotten where she was. Then color rose in her cheeks. She straightened quickly, brushing dirt from her sleeve.","\"You’re awake.\"","\"Ah.. Yeah.\"","She studied his face. \"Do you remember anything?\"","\"Last clear thing is hitting my head on that tree and going down the slope. After that it’s pieces. You pulling me. Screams. Not much else.\"","She nodded. \"That’s about right.\"","Rael shifted closer, careful of the low ceiling. \"Thank you for getting me here. I owe you for that. I’ll make it up to you when I can.\"","\"You don’t owe me. You caught me on the way down. I just dragged you the rest of the way. We’re even.\"","\"Still. Thank you.\"","He was quiet a moment, listening to the faint wind outside the blocked entrance. \"How long have we been here?\"","\"I’m not sure. More than five days, I think. You had a fever while you were out the whole time. I applied medicine on your wound but the bleeding didn’t stop until yesterday.\"","\"Five days?\" The number hit harder than the tree. He had believed it was one day, maybe two. Five.","Lila’s voice dropped. \"When I was pulling you toward this place I saw others. I heard them killing others. The mountain was covered with corpses. Monster and High-level at that. Not just the wolf. Several of them moving through the trees. That’s why I stayed put. Moving felt worse than waiting.\"","Rael sat with that. One frost dire wolf had already been an anomaly. Multiple high-level monsters in the first realm was something else entirely. He looked at the narrow walls, the carefully blocked entrance, the thin light filtering through the branches","He felt trapped. When he got the class he thought all his problems are gone away but now it’s like whole other dangers has come to him. Rael was sure of one thing he doesn’t want to die here just when he started to walk towards his dream.","\"So how do we get out of here?\" he muttered. \"Feels like we’re sitting inside a giant cage.\"","---"]},{"number":9,"title":"Chapter 9: Eagel Eye","paragraphs":["Ten days later ~","I don’t know what happened, and I can’t recall how I got here. But what I’m seeing right now is no longer any world I ever imagined.","I moved my head and the first thing that registered was the smell — copper and rot, thick enough to coat the back of my throat before my eyes had even finished adjusting. Then the bodies. Hundreds, maybe thousands, stacked in loose piles that rose higher than a man, slumped against each other in postures no living body ever holds. Every single one was missing its head. The floor beneath the piles was a continuous sheet of red, so deep and so old in places that it no longer looked like liquid at all. It looked like paint that had dried, cracked open, and been painted over again — layer after layer of the same dark color, a record of how many times this had happened before.","The silence was the worst part. No flies. No dripping. No wind. Just the faint, wet click of something moving in the middle of it all.","In the center sat a girl. Her white skirt had been soaked through so completely that the fabric no longer draped — it had stiffened into dark, crimson folds, cracking faintly at the knee every time she shifted, like something dried around her rather than worn. In one hand she held a severed head by its hair, tilted back at an angle, the neck still slick and glistening where it had been torn rather than cut. Her other hand was buried inside the skull, fingers working with slow, practiced patience through a wound at the crown. Her face was streaked with blood in careless smears — across her cheek, down her jaw, one thin arc flicked over her brow like she hadn’t even noticed it landing there. She lifted a piece of grey-pink matter to her mouth and ate it with the same quiet, unhurried focus most people give a meal they’ve been waiting far too long for. Her jaw worked slowly. Her eyes stayed half-lidded, almost peaceful, as if the thousand heads around her weren’t there at all — as if this were the only clean, private moment she had all day.","She hummed. Softly. Off-key. A child’s tune, the kind hummed absentmindedly while doing something completely ordinary.","Rael’s legs gave a violent shake beneath him, his knees threatening to buckle before he even registered standing. \"What in the world is happening?\"","Present ~","Rael eased the bushes aside a finger’s width and peered out. From this angle he could see almost nothing—just a narrow slice of slope and the grey-green blur of trees lower down. He pushed the branches farther and started to crawl out.","Lila’s hand closed around his wrist.","\"Don’t. It’s dangerous.\"","\"We have less than two days of food left,\" he said. \"If we stay sealed in here we’ll starve before any monster finds us. I only need to go a little way. Check the ground. Come straight back.\"","Her face tightened. He saw the fear there, clear and unhidden.","\"All right,\" he said, softer. \"I’ll stay close. Five minutes. Ten at most.\"","She shook her head. \"Wait. I’m coming with you.\"","He looked at her. \"You sure?\"","\"Yes. You’re right. We can’t live inside this hole forever. We have to leave sometime.\"","They were higher on the mountain than Rael had realised. He still had no idea how she had managed to drag his dead weight this far up while he was unconscious. The two of them moved in a low crouch, keeping to the broken rock and the patches of scrub that offered at least a little cover. The air was colder here, thinner, carrying the faint metallic smell of distant blood.","Lila stopped first. She pointed with her chin.","\"There. Orc. Huh it’s weird.\"","Rael squinted. He saw nothing but trees and shadow. Then he remembered—archer class. He reached out and laid his fingers lightly on her shoulder.","\"Hey...\"","She flinched at the contact, then realised he was not looking at her. She turned her attention back to the slope and said nothing.","[Class detected]","[Class: Archer (C-Rank)]","[Do you want to copy?]","[Y/N]","He clicked yes.","[Archer] (C-Rank → B-Rank)","[A ranged combat class specialising in bows and precision attacks. Archers rely on sharp senses, accuracy, agility, and powerful ranged attacks to defeat enemies from a distance.]","[Skills]","[Eagle Eyes] (Apprentice → Expert)","[Enhances the user’s eyesight, allowing them to see targets clearly from extreme distances. It improves accuracy, perception, and target tracking, making it easier to identify weak points and hit moving targets.]","[Piercing Arrow] (Apprentice → Expert)","[Fires an arrow with concentrated force that can pierce through enemies, armour, and weaker defences. The greater the user’s strength and mastery, the more powerful the arrow becomes.]","The skill settled into him like a second set of eyes opening. The world sharpened. Distances that had been vague shapes resolved into hard detail.","It was the first time in his life he had seen this far with such clarity. Every leaf, every drop of blood on the undergrowth, every twitch of a dying hand registered with painful sharpness. The scale of it settled over him slowly. This was not a hunting ground. It was a collection.","He followed the line of Lila’s gaze and found the creature almost at once.","An orc, ten feet tall at least, moved through the trees with a slow, deliberate gait. In one massive hand it dragged a human body by the ankle. The body was still alive. Rael could see the chest rising and falling in shallow, panicked breaths, the hands clawing weakly at the dirt. The orc did not look down. It simply walked, as if the living weight behind it were no more than a sack of grain.","Rael had studied the bestiary records before the ceremony. Orcs of that size were almost always level twenty or a little higher. They did not belong in the first awakening realm. Nothing above level ten was supposed to exist here. Yet the creature moved with the easy confidence of something that had never been told the rules.","He turned his head a few degrees and the enhanced sight kept giving him more. A pack of wolves—lean, grey, larger than any natural breed—trotted in single file across a clearing. Each of them held a living person in its jaws, the bodies hanging limp but not yet dead. One of the captives still tried to scream; the sound came out as a wet, muffled cry before the wolf’s teeth shifted and silenced it.","Farther to the right a second orc appeared, then a third. Between the trees he caught glimpses of other shapes—things with too many limbs, things that moved on all fours but had the silhouettes of men, things that left trails of frost wherever they stepped. All of them carried living prey. None of them stopped to feed.","A white flash cut across his vision. When the dust settled he recognised the shape at once—the same frost dire wolf they had fled days earlier. The beast stood on a ridge of broken stone, head raised, nostrils working the air. For a second it seemed only to be scenting the wind. Then its head snapped around and its eyes locked directly on the place where Rael and Lila crouched.","Rael dropped flat and pulled Lila down with him. They pressed themselves into the rock, hardly breathing.","\"Back,\" he whispered. \"Now.\"","She did not ask questions. They retreated the way they had come, moving only when the wolf’s attention seemed to slide elsewhere, freezing whenever its head turned. The cave mouth finally appeared between the bushes. They slipped inside and Lila dragged the branches back into place until the entrance was sealed again.","In the dim light Rael sat with his back against the stone wall, the after-image of what he had seen still burning behind his eyes. The behaviour of the beasts made no sense. Orcs and wolves of that kind did not store food. They killed and ate on the spot. Keeping humans alive, dragging them somewhere out of sight—that was something else.","And the levels. Too many high-level creatures in a realm that should have held nothing above ten. The only explanation that fitted was the one that felt most absurd: someone had summoned them. Someone had brought second-realm beasts into the first awakening ground on purpose.","He looked at Lila across the narrow space. Neither of them spoke for a long time. Outside, the mountain continued its quiet work of hiding whatever was being gathered in the trees below.","---"]},{"number":10,"title":"Chapter 10: Realms","paragraphs":["Rael sat with his back against the rough stone and went over everything he knew about the realms one more time, checking each fact against the theory forming in his head.","(Author Note: Info Dump)","First Realm — Elysian Wilds.","People called it the tutorial stage. Every newly awakened person was sent here a month after the ceremony. There were no cities, no safe zones, no reliable roads. The place was nothing but wilderness, monsters, and dangerous ground. The only way out was a Realm Gate hidden somewhere in the wild. Until an awakener found that gate, there was no return to Titan. The gate’s location changed for every group, forcing them to explore and survive.","Second Realm — Aetherion.","This was where most awakeners spent their lives. Unlike the first realm it had human cities, kingdoms, guilds, markets, academies and dungeons. Cities connected through Safe Portals and Realm Gates, so people could travel between major settlements without crossing open wilderness. Outside the walls, though, the monsters were still strong.","Third Realm — Eternal Abyss.","The place beyond ordinary reach. Only the strongest ever entered. Legendary monsters, forbidden treasures, extinct civilizations, powers that should no longer exist. No ordinary cities. No safe transport. The few who returned spoke of things that sounded impossible. Most people believed the Eternal Abyss was the highest realm of the Dream Realm. That was only what humanity believed.","Rael finished the silent recitation and looked across the narrow cave at Lila.","\"I think I have an idea why this is happening,\" he said. \"Why monsters like Dire Wolf and Orcs are in the first realm at all.\"","She waited.","\"There’s a summoner here. Someone with a high-rank class—A or S, maybe—who summoned all these creatures in. It’s the only way so many of them could appear at once.\"","He waited till look at Lira’s reaction and said \"If we kill the summoner, the summons might disappear with him.\"","Lila was quiet for a moment, then nodded slowly. \"That... actually makes sense.\"","\"We can’t touch him the way we are now,\" Rael continued. \"We’re too weak. We have to get stronger first, and fast.\"","\"We have to make our move before these monsters find us.\"","\"Then how about we leave the cave tomorrow morning,\" she said. \"Start with low-level monsters. Build from there.\"","\"No I think we should move at night. If a human is controlling them they have to sleep at night. It will be the best time to move we can rest at day and move at night.\"","She leaned her head back against the wall. \"Yeah. Let’s do that.\"","She shifted and sat beside him, close enough that their shoulders almost touched. For a while neither spoke. Then she said, voice low, \"You know I didn’t trust you at first. When you said you forgot the ring... I didn’t believe that story.\"","Rael gave a short, tired breath that was almost a laugh. \"I wouldn’t have believed me either if I were you.\"","A small smile touched her mouth. \"So what really happened? Why did you not brought any equipment?\"","\"I failed the awakening. I didn’t know I was going to awaken,\" he said. \"The stone told me I was classless. Turns out I wasn’t. After coming here I got to know I do have a class.\"","She studied his face in the dim light. \"Then you must be glad. Having a class. Being here.\"","He nodded once. He said \"I have never been this happy before.\"","Lila looked down at her hands. \"I never wanted to awaken. I wanted a normal life.\"","\"Why? Isn’t every kids dream is to become a hero.\"","She was silent long enough that he thought she might not answer. When she did, the words came slowly.","\"My parents died in a gate outbreak. Left me, my sister, and my older brother. We were not much older when our parents died My brother took care of us. He later awakened with an A-rank class. He was the pride of our university. Big guilds came after him, but his dream was to build his own in his own city. He joined a party from a famous guild to raise money for it. He never came back. They said he died in the dungeon.\"","A tear slid down her cheek. She did not wipe it away.","\"I see my sister cry every night when she thinks she’s alone. Our family was never the same after that. And the thought of me dying here too... I keep thinking what that would do to her. Once I escape the first realm I’m never coming back here.\"","She reached up, unclasped the thin star shaped necklace from around her neck, and held it out to him.","\"If anything happens to me and you get out, give this to my sister. Tell her to keep living even when it’s tough. Even when it looks like there is nothing to live for any longer.\"","Rael did not take it at once. \"You’ll give it to her yourself. Nothing’s going to happen to you while I’m here.\"","Rael felt weird saying that he knew how weak he was right now. How can he protect her when he can’t even guarantee his own safety.","She pressed the necklace into his palm anyway and closed his fingers over it. \"Even then. I want you to have it.\"","He looked at the small chain resting against his skin, then at her face, still wet with the single tear. He closed his hand gently around the necklace.","\"All right,\" he said. \"But you’re still giving it to her yourself.\"","They sat in the quiet that followed. Outside the blocked entrance the mountain wind moved through the trees, carrying no sound of the creatures below.","\"Let’s rest,\" Rael said at last. \"We’ll move at night. When the summoner’s asleep his control should loosen. That might be our best chance.\"","Lila nodded. She did not take her hand away from his for a long time. Rael looked at his status screen.","STATUS","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Copied Class: Flamecaller(D-Rank), Archer (B-Rank)","Level: 3 -> 4","Souls: 20 -> 40","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier)","Strength: 26","Agility: 26","Mana: 26","Vitality: 26","Additional Stats: 6","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert), Eagel Eyes (Expert), Piercing Arrow (Expert)","He thought he has to grow stronger, much stronger if he can have confidence when he tell someone that he will protect them.","---"]},{"number":11,"title":"Chapter 11: Level Up","paragraphs":["Night had already settled over the mountain when Rael stepped out of the cave. The last thin light had drained from the sky, leaving only the hard blue-black of true dark. He carried Nathan’s wand in his right hand and a short goblin blade in his left. Lila followed at a distance, bow already strung, melting into the trees and undergrowth the way only an archer could.","They had talked it through earlier. No more low-level scraps if they could help it. They needed real growth, real pressure. Rael remember he never checked the discription of Nathan’s Wand.","Rael paused long enough to examine the wand properly.","Name: Emberwood Wand","Tier: Common","Description: A simple wooden wand made from enchanted emberwood. It slightly increases the user’s Mana Control and makes fire-based spells easier to cast. A reliable weapon for beginner mages.","Effect: Mana Control +5% | Fire Spell Power +5%","Lowest tier possible. Yet the bonuses mattered. Fireball and Ignite were already his main tools. Five percent more power was five percent less chance of dying.","They moved in silence for a long time before the smell hit them—raw meat and wet fur. Ahead, in a shallow depression between two rocks, a horned rabbit tore into the carcass of another of its kind. Three feet long from nose to haunch, thick-muscled, a single black horn jutting from its forehead like a spear tip. Level 4.","Rael raised a hand. Lila stopped and waited in the dark. He wanted this one himself.","He stepped into the open and pointed the wand.","\"Fireball.\"","A sphere of orange fire roared out. The rabbit’s head snapped up an instant before impact. It threw itself sideways, but the edge of the blast still caught the right side of its face. Fur blackened. Skin split and blistered. The animal screamed—a high, wet sound that did not belong to anything that should have been called a rabbit.","Lila’s arrow arrived a heartbeat later. It punched into the burned side of the head, burying itself almost to the fletching. The rabbit staggered, thrashing, horn swinging wildly. Rael closed the distance in three hard strides. The goblin blade went in under the jaw and up through the soft palate into the brain. He felt the tip grind against bone. He twisted. The thrashing stopped. Hot blood poured over his wrist.","[+3 Souls]","He pulled the blade free with a wet sound. The body slumped. He had liked cute things once. This was not cute. This was a three-foot predator that ate its own kind and would have driven that horn through his gut without hesitation.","He gestured. Lila came forward. They stored the corpse in the storage ring—his for now, so he could carry the dead without slowing them. Then they moved on.","The next clearing was worse.","More than fifty slimes rested in a loose colony across damp stone and moss. Level 3 each. Soft, translucent, faintly glowing. They looked almost peaceful until you remembered what they did to anything that stepped into their territory—dissolve flesh, absorb bone, leave nothing but wet stains.","Rael stood at the edge and felt the old reluctance rise. He crushed it. This world did not care about reluctance. Kill or be killed. Half-measures got you eaten.","He raised the wand and called Ignite.","Mana circles bloomed under the nearest cluster of slimes like sudden brands. The creatures stirred, sensing the change a second too late. He flicked the wand. Fire erupted from the ground in a continuous sheet. The first slime ballooned, skin blackening, then burst with a wet pop. Thick, burning fluid sprayed across its neighbors. They tried to crawl away. The fire followed, clinging, eating.","Rael did not stop. He walked forward, wand hand steady, calling the skill again and again. New circles. New bursts. The air filled with the stench of cooked slime—sweet and chemical and wrong. Lila’s arrows hissed past him, each one finding a core with clean precision. One shot, one kill. The rest she left to the fire.","A slime the size of a man’s torso rolled toward Rael’s boots, surface already smoking. He stepped back and drove a Fireball straight into its center. The explosion painted the rocks with glowing fragments. Another came from the side. He met it with the goblin blade, cutting deep. The blade sank into jelly that clung and tried to climb his arm. He ignited the blade itself with a short pulse of Ignite and watched the slime cook from the inside out, bubbling and collapsing in a heap of blackened sludge.","The colony tried to scatter. Some of the smaller ones rolled into the undergrowth. Rael chased them with Fireball after Fireball, the wand growing warm in his grip. Each impact lit the night for a moment—orange flash, wet detonation, the wet slap of dissolving flesh against stone. Lila never wasted an arrow. When a slime tried to climb a tree she put a shaft through its core and watched it drip down the bark in smoking ropes.","Rael’s lungs burned from the smoke and the constant casting. Sweat ran into his eyes. He wiped it away with the back of a blood-slick hand and kept moving. The last large slime tried to flee toward deeper shadow. He caught it with a final Ignite circle and watched it thrash as the fire climbed through its body from the bottom up. It made a high, keening sound that faded only when nothing remained but a greasy scorch mark.","Notifications flickered at the edge of his vision in a steady stream.","[+2 Souls]","[+2 Souls]","[+2 Souls]","He did not like what he was doing. The dislike sat in his chest like a stone. He ignored it. This was the rule of the place. Commit fully or die halfway.","When the last slime burst he felt the familiar pressure behind his eyes.","[+ Level Up]","He checked the status.","STATUS","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Copied Classes: Flamecaller (C-Rank), Archer (B-Rank)","Level: 4 → 5","Souls: 40 → 97","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Attributes:","Strength: 28","Agility: 32","Mana: 32","Vitality: 28","Additional Stats: 0","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert), Eagle Eyes (Expert), Piercing Arrow (Expert)","He poured the free points into Mana and Agility—the two stats he was burning through every fight. The change settled into him like a deeper breath.","Lila stepped out of the trees, bow still half-drawn. She looked at him once and said, \"You leveled up.\"","\"Yeah.\" His voice came out rough. \"I think I killed more of them than I meant to.\"","She glanced at the scorched clearing—black patches, wet remains, the sharp chemical smell still hanging in the air. She said nothing else.","Rael opened the soul menu. He could upgrade skills or the Copy class itself. The skills would vanish when the copied classes expired in a month. No point wasting souls there. He focused on Copy.","[Class: Copy (F-Rank) — Upgrade]","[Copy (F-Rank) → Analyze (E-Rank)]","[Souls Required: 97 / 1000]","Almost a thousand. Far beyond what they had. He closed the window.","The night pressed in around them again. Somewhere farther down the mountain something large moved through the trees, branches cracking under its weight. Rael wiped the goblin blade on a patch of moss, sheathed it, and looked at Lila.","They still had a long way to go before the summons stopped coming.","---"]},{"number":12,"title":"Chapter 12: Ape","paragraphs":["They rested only long enough for the worst of the exhaustion to ease, then left again under full dark. The night stretched long. Goblins came first—small, hunched things with yellow eyes that reflected torchlight that was not there. Rael and Lila cut them down without ceremony. Later the rats appeared, the size of large dogs, teeth yellow and wet. Those died much quicker. By the time the sky began to pale they had left a trail of small corpses behind them and the storage ring felt heavier.","Dawn was only a thin grey line when they turned back toward the cave. The air smelled of wet earth and distant smoke. Rael walked ahead, wand in one hand, goblin blade in the other. Lila stayed farther back among the trees, an arrow already nocked.","ROOOAAARRR!","The roar came without warning.","It rolled through the forest like something heavy and wet being torn open. Both of them froze, then turned. Branches exploded outward. A massive shape burst from the undergrowth at full charge.","[Forest Ape]","[Level 24]","[Rank: Savage]","It was easily twelve feet tall, its body a dense mass of black-brown fur and knotted muscle. Long arms hung nearly to the ground. Its mouth hung open, ropes of thick saliva swinging with every stride. Small, furious eyes locked on them.","Rael pushed Lila out of the way of the beast. The ape reached Rael. One enormous hand swung sideways and caught him across the chest. The impact lifted him clean off his feet and hurled him through the air. He slammed into a thick trunk hard enough to make the wood crack. Air left his lungs in a single wet burst. He slid down the bark and landed on all fours, coughing.","Lila’s legs were shaking. The ape turned toward her. Spit dripped from its jaws onto the leaves. She reached for another arrow. The ape kicked her before the shaft cleared the quiver. The blow sent her tumbling across the ground. She tried to rise. The creature closed the distance in two strides, closed one massive hand around her skull, and lifted her off the ground as if she weighed nothing.","AAAAAHHHH!","Lila screamed. The sound was high and raw. The ape’s fingers began to tighten.","Rael forced himself upright. Blood ran from the corner of his mouth. He had no time for a proper arrow. He snatched a sharp stone from the ground, focused, and poured Piercing Arrow into it. The stone left his hand like a bullet and struck the ape square in the temple.","It turned to dust on contact.","The ape dropped Lila and charged. Four limbs drove it forward with terrifying speed. Rael threw himself sideways. The creature smashed into the tree he had just left. The trunk shattered. Splinters the size of spears exploded outward. Rael rolled, came up on one knee, and flung a Fireball at the ape’s chest. The flame washed across thick fur and only scorched the outer layer. The smell of burned hair filled the air.","The ape grabbed his leg, swung him like a club, and released. Rael’s body hit another tree with enough force to snap it clean through. He hit the ground hard and coughed a mouthful of bright blood onto the dirt. Pain radiated through every rib. He had believed the class would make him strong. The world was reminding him how little that strength still meant.","The ape raised one massive foot to stomp. Rael twisted. The foot came down on his side instead of his head. His vitality held. Bones flexed but did not break. The pressure still drove the air from his lungs and left black spots swimming across his vision.","Lila pushed herself up. Blood covered half her face from a cut above the eye. She looked at the ape crushing Rael into the ground and for one clear second the thought of running crossed her mind. The ape was focused. She could disappear into the trees. No one would know what happened here.","She gritted her teeth until her jaw ached, raised the bow, and loosed.","The arrow sank deep into the ape’s supporting leg. The creature roared and staggered. Its stomp went wide and slammed into empty earth hard enough to leave a crater. Dirt and stones sprayed outward.","The ape’s head snapped toward Lila. It abandoned Rael and charged her on three limbs, the wounded leg dragging. It did not try to strike this time. It simply closed both hands around her entire body and lifted her again. Lila’s feet left the ground. Tears cut clean lines through the blood on her cheeks. In that moment she only saw her sister’s face and the empty house that would wait if she never returned.","Rael forced himself to his feet. Every breath scraped. He ran whole pulling his right leg with him. The goblin blade went into the ape’s lower back with both hands behind it. The thick muscle resisted, then gave. The ape bellowed and spun, still holding Lila. Rael did not let go of the blade. The Ape used his hand to remove Rael from his bCk but he climbed the creature’s spine using the embedded sword as an anchor, reached the thick neck, and drove the Emberwood Wand straight into the soft tissue behind the skull.","\"Fireball.\"","The spell detonated inside the flesh.","The ape’s neck erupted outward in a wet explosion of bone, muscle, and burning blood. The head lolled sideways, half-severed. The hands released Lila. She fell hard. The massive body swayed once, then collapsed forward like a falling tree, shaking the ground when it hit.","Rael dropped with it, still gripping the wand. Steam rose from the ruined neck. The forest fell quiet except for their ragged breathing and the slow drip of blood from the open wound.","He stayed on his knees for a long moment, chest heaving, vision still edged with black. Lila crawled the short distance to him and sat with her back against the same fallen body, both of them too spent to speak.","The first true light of morning filtered through the broken canopy above them.","[+ Level up]","---"]},{"number":13,"title":"Chapter 13: Emotions","paragraphs":["His wand was gone — not broken, shredded, a branch torn apart by something that had never once slowed down for him. His palms were blistered raw where it had detonated in his grip, too close, too late. He didn’t feel the pain yet. That would come later, when his body decided it had permission.","The notifications kept arriving long after the ape had stopped moving. Long after the forest had gone quiet in that specific, ringing way that only follows something enormous dying.","[+ Level Up]","[+ Soul obtained]","[+ Level Up]","[+ Soul obtained]","They stacked across his vision until the screen blurred into a wall of light. Rael stared through them, not at them. Somewhere in the flood, the truth finally landed: a level 5 awakener had killed a Savage-rank beast. An ant had killed an elephant and was still standing on its legs to prove it. No archive, no tutor, no bedtime story from the old war veterans had ever described anything like it. The fact didn’t feel like triumph. It sat in his chest like a stone dropped into still water — too large, too heavy, refusing to stop rippling.","He forced the status window open with hands that wouldn’t stop shaking.","Status","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Copied Classes: Flamecaller (C-Rank), Archer (B-Rank)","Level: 5 → 9","Souls: 97 → 568","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Attributes","Strength: 36","Agility: 40","Mana: 40","Vitality: 36","Additional Stats: 8","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert), Eagle Eyes (Expert), Piercing Arrow (Expert)","The numbers had climbed like something starving finally being fed. His body hadn’t caught up to them. It still felt exactly as broken as it had a minute ago — because it was. He planted his palms in the blood-flattened grass and pushed himself upright, and his right leg buckled the instant it took weight, a white flash of pain shooting up to his hip. He locked his jaw and locked the joint and made himself stand anyway. Every breath dragged against something wrong in his ribs, a grinding little reminder that he was not, in fact, invincible. Just lucky. Or maybe not even that.","Then he looked at Lila, and the numbers stopped mattering entirely.","She hadn’t moved an inch since the fight ended. Blood matted one side of her face, dark and drying at the temple. Both arms hung at angles that made his stomach turn just to see. She could have run. The ape had wanted him, only him, and she’d had a clean line to the tree cover the whole time. She could have vanished and lived and no one would have blamed her — he wouldn’t have blamed her. She had stayed instead. Standing over him, drawing an arrow, buying him the half-second he needed.","That realization hit harder than any of the level-ups. Harder than the bloodline notification. Harder than anything the screen had shown him.","He slid his arms beneath her, careful of the arms that weren’t supposed to bend the way they bent, and lifted. A princess carry was the only hold that wouldn’t make her scream in whatever place she was currently hiding from the pain. His own leg screamed instead, buckling on the first step, and he locked it again and kept moving because stopping wasn’t a choice that existed right now. Open ground was a death sentence. If the summoner who’d sent that ape realized two half-dead survivors were sitting beside its corpse, every leashed beast in a mile radius would come looking. So he walked. Slow, stubborn, refusing steps, the only rhythm his body had left to offer — carrying her toward the cave like it was the only decision he’d ever been sure of.","He thought, absurdly, of the noble houses. How they trained for this exact night for years before it ever arrived — how children were sent into the Dream Realm in groups, flanked by branch families and alliance houses who’d drilled contingencies since before those children could walk. Supply caches. Assigned roles. Rituals of readiness passed down like inheritance. If the stone had given him any ordinary class, he’d have stepped into the first realm surrounded by people who already knew his name, his weaknesses, his blind spots. Instead he was here — dragging a dying girl through undergrowth on a leg that wouldn’t hold, an F-rank Copy class limping through the dark like a joke the universe hadn’t finished telling yet. The first awakening was never supposed to be this hard. He turned the thought over and over and found no answer waiting on the other side of it.","The cave mouth opened up between the bushes, black and narrow and, for the moment, safe enough. He lowered Lila onto the stone as gently as his shaking arms allowed and knelt beside her, and for a second he just knelt there, breathing, because moving again meant admitting how little time she might have left. Without treatment she would die. That thought moved him faster than fear ever could. He pried open her storage ring with fingers that fumbled twice before they worked, and searched until his hand closed around a single glass vial.","Health potion","A liquid that closes wounds and knits bone at a speed no ordinary medicine can match.","He could have drunk it himself. He was in no better shape than she was, and no one on earth would have blamed him for choosing his own survival — that was simply how the world worked, how it had always worked for people who weren’t born with a name that opened doors. Instead he tipped it carefully between her lips, one slow swallow at a time, willing none of it to spill. She drank without waking. The worst of the gashes along her scalp began to knit shut before his eyes. The wrong angles of her arms shifted, resettled, corrected themselves with small wet sounds he tried not to listen to. Color crept back into her face in careful degrees, like dawn deciding whether the day was worth it. She still didn’t open her eyes. Her body had simply chosen to rest, trusting that someone would still be there when it woke.","He looked at the empty vial in his palm for a long moment. The recipe was locked behind royal decree, guarded tighter than most bloodlines. Even the great houses couldn’t brew it themselves — only the Solaria family held the right, under strict quota, at a price that had quietly buried the old hospital trade a generation ago. One vial was a small fortune. Lila had carried exactly one, and she had spent it on a boy she’d known for less than a season.","He understood, with sudden and unbearable clarity, exactly what that meant.","Silence filled the cave after that — total, complete, with no one left to break it. It pressed into every place still hurting: his ribs, his leg, the raw place behind his eyes where exhaustion had started pooling hours ago and never drained. He thought of home. The long stone corridor he used to race down as a boy. Sparring with his siblings until the sun went down. Laughing with friends who’d sworn nothing would ever change between them. For years he had carried the quiet certainty that Blackthorn blood did not fail — that whatever happened, the name would hold him up. That certainty had died the moment his awakening stone stayed dark in front of the whole hall. And in the hollow it left behind sat a colder, uglier question, one he hadn’t let himself ask until now: how fast they had all moved on. Not slow, not reluctant — fast. No message since. No name called into the dark. Just a door closing, quietly, like he’d never been expected to walk back through it.","\"Mother,\" he said, to no one, his eyes fixed on nothing.","The word left him thinner than he meant it to. A single tear cut a clean line through the dirt on his cheek before he could stop it, and he let it fall. He had spent years perfecting a face that didn’t flinch — through the humiliation, through the failure, through the public silence that followed a failed awakening like a smell that never quite left the room. He had absorbed all of it without once letting the crack show, had told himself he was building toward the day he could say, truthfully, that he didn’t need their love anymore. That day had not come. The crack was showing now, and for the first time in longer than he could remember, he didn’t try to close it. All he had ever actually wanted — underneath the training, the ambition, the desperate hunger to become strong enough to matter — was to be loved by the people he loved. Not power for its own sake. Not vengeance. Just his family, beside him, the way it used to be. Especially her.","\"I miss you,\" he said, quieter now, the words barely making it past his throat. \"Was it fake too? Was your love fake too?\"","No answer came back. He hadn’t expected one. Some part of him had been asking that question into empty rooms for weeks now, and some part of him already understood that the answer, whatever it was, would not be coming from this cave, tonight, or maybe ever.","He lowered himself down beside Lila, close enough that their shoulders touched, and took her hand in his. He told himself it was so she wouldn’t wake alone in the dark, in a strange cave, with no memory of how she’d gotten there. The quieter, truer reason was that he didn’t want to feel the empty stretch of stone on his own side of the cave — didn’t want one more night of reaching for something that wasn’t there. She had stayed when leaving would have cost her nothing. That single fact was the only ground under him that still felt solid.","He closed his eyes. The last thing he registered, before sleep dragged him under, was the slow, steady warmth of her fingers curled against his — proof, small and stubborn, that not everyone left. Then sleep took him, heavy and without dreams.","---"]},{"number":14,"title":"Chapter 14: Kaia Blackthorn","paragraphs":["The press room was already full when the lights came up, but it was not the ordinary fullness of a scheduled briefing. It was the fullness of history waiting to be recorded. Rows of reporters sat packed shoulder to shoulder, tablets hovering in mid-air, recorder crystals pulsing a steady blue, the low murmur of last-minute strategy hanging over the room like smoke that refused to clear. Outside the tall windows of the Solaria Guild Hall, the skyline burned gold under an artificial dusk — towers of mana-glass catching the light and throwing it back in long, trembling reflections, as if the entire city itself were holding its breath along with the room. Every seat faced the raised platform and the single microphone that waited beneath a soft white beam, small and unremarkable, utterly unworthy of what it was about to broadcast to every corner of Solaria.","This was not merely the biggest story of the season. It was the story of a generation. Eclipse guild had done what forty years of the strongest parties in the Second Realm had failed to do — they had cleared Dragon’s Grave, an S-rank dungeon so notorious that guild masters spoke its name the way sailors once spoke of drowned cities. Three hundred and eleven attempts. Three hundred and eleven failures, some of them total wipes, entire elite parties reduced to obituary lists nailed to guild hall doors. And at the black, ash-choked heart of that dungeon had coiled the Black Dragon — a being old enough to remember when the Second Realm had no cities at all, powerful enough that its name alone had been enough to keep S-rank guilds from even attempting the depths for over a decade.","Eclipse had killed it. And not a single member of their party had died doing it.","In a world where even champion-tier parties expected to bury someone after an S-rank clear, that single fact alone was enough to make an entire press room forget how to sit still.","A door at the side of the platform opened, and the room did not so much quiet as it collapsed into silence, all at once, like a held breath finally released the wrong way.","Kaia Blackthorn walked in without hurry.","She wore the formal black of Eclipse’s leadership uniform, tailored close, the silver crest of the guild burning faintly at her collar with its own inner light. Her hair was tied back with the same exact, merciless precision she gave to everything that mattered — and to a woman like Kaia, everything mattered. She was the eldest daughter of House Blackthorn, one of the seven Sovereign families of the kingdom, co-leader of a guild that had just rewritten the record books, and — depending on which gossip feed one trusted least — the single most watched young woman in the Solaria kingdom. Even among a press corps hardened by a thousand jaded briefings, there was a short, collective intake of breath as she crossed the stage. It was not merely her beauty, though that alone could have stopped traffic in three kingdoms. It was the weight she carried into a room. Mana rolled off her in a thin, disciplined current, barely perceptible, like standing near a sheathed blade and feeling the cold of the metal anyway.","She stepped up to the microphone, tapped it twice, waited for the soft confirmation chime, and let her eyes pass once, slowly, across the entire room — a silent warning disguised as a formality.","\"We’ll answer questions related to Dragon’s Grave.\" Her voice was even, flat, carved from something colder than politeness. \"Nothing outside that scope.\"","A heavy-set man in the front row shot to his feet before the words had finished landing. \"Is it true your team killed the Black Dragon without losing a single member?\"","Kaia’s gaze swept the room once more. \"Count our roster yourselves if you’d like. We’re all still standing.\"","The man smiled, trying — and failing — for lightness. \"What I meant was the overall cost. How badly did Eclipse come out of it?\"","\"Minor injuries.\" A pause, precise as a blade stroke. \"Nothing that will slow our next schedule.\"","A woman with short black hair rose next, pen already moving before Kaia had finished speaking. \"How long did the clear take?\"","\"A little over two months.\" Something flickered behind Kaia’s eyes for half a heartbeat — the memory of sixty-one days without full sleep, of watching her vice-captain nearly burn out his mana core holding the boss’s attention, of the moment the dragon’s roar had cracked three support pillars and half the room had genuinely believed they were about to die together in the dark. None of it touched her voice.","\"And the corpse?\" the same reporter pressed. \"There are rumors Lionheart has already opened talks to buy the remains.\"","The room leaned in as one organism.","Kaia let the silence stretch — not out of hesitation, but because she wanted every recorder in the building to catch exactly how absolute her answer was. \"We’re keeping the materials. Every scale, every claw, every drop of ichor. They’ll be used to arm and armor this guild, not sold to line someone else’s treasury.\"","A murmur went through the room like wind through dry grass. Lionheart was the single largest guild south of the capital. To refuse them so openly, so publicly, was not merely a business decision — it was a declaration.","More questions followed in a rapid, hungry volley — loot distribution, which wing of the dungeon had broken the most bodies, whether Eclipse intended to publish a full after-action report for the Guild Registry. Kaia answered each one in short, exact sentences, never once expanding past what was asked, never once allowing warmth to creep into her tone. The role of public face had fallen onto her shoulders years ago, back when she was barely seventeen and already burying classmates, and she performed it now the way she performed everything: with total control and not one wasted motion.","Then the golden-haired reporter rose.","She was the kind who usually chased scandal rather than dungeon clears — perfume a little too strong, smile a little too rehearsed, the sort of journalist who had built a career out of catching royalty mid-stumble. Her smile was already fixed in place before she’d even finished standing.","\"Miss Blackthorn — is it true you and Eros Strombane are dating?\"","Kaia did not look at her. She simply turned toward the next raised hand, as smoothly and completely as if the question had dissolved in the air before it ever reached her ears. A ripple of uncomfortable laughter moved through the back rows. The golden-haired reporter remained standing, color rising hot in her cheeks, humiliation and stubbornness warring visibly across her face.","The remaining dungeon questions came and went quickly after that, each one closed the same clean way — factual, clipped, final. Kaia was already half-turned toward the side door, the conference all but over in her mind, when the golden-haired reporter spoke again — louder this time, and with the reckless edge of someone who had decided that if she couldn’t win with charm, she’d win with a blade instead.","\"Miss Kaia — the nation wants to know. Your brother, Rael Blackthorn, was confirmed classless and has been missing for over a month. What do you have to say about him?\"","The temperature in the room did not merely drop. It fell, all at once, like a stone dropped down a well with no bottom. Every recorder kept running, their little blue lights the only thing in the room still moving. No one dared breathe past that first frozen inhale. Even the reporters who had spent years chasing the worst of the noble houses understood, in that instant, that they had just watched someone step onto ground that should never, ever have been touched in public.","Kaia turned back to the microphone. Slowly. Completely.","She did not raise her voice.","She did not need to.","The mana that had been coiled so tightly beneath her skin for the entire conference unspooled all at once — not an explosion, nothing so crude, but a vast, silent pressure that rolled outward from the stage like the tide going out before a wave no one had been warned about. The temperature of the room dropped hard enough that breath began fogging in front of startled faces. Recorder crystals flickered and dimmed, their delicate enchantments straining against a will that had not even been aimed at them. Somewhere near the back, a full pitcher of water sitting untouched on a press table sheeted over with a thin, silent skin of frost.","Kaia simply looked at the reporter.","That was all. She did not move. She did not speak. She only looked, with the full, unblinking weight of someone who had personally ended the life of a being old enough to remember when dragons still ruled uncontested. The golden-haired reporter’s rehearsed smile did not so much fade as shatter, piece by piece, under a gaze that made a decade-old S-rank dragon’s death feel like a minor Tuesday afternoon. Her legs began to shake, visibly, audibly, her heels drumming a frantic, involuntary rhythm against the floor.","Security was already moving before Kaia’s stare reached its second full second — two guild-hall officers half-running down the aisle, faces pale, clearly more afraid of what might happen if they were too slow than of the reporter herself. They took her by the arms and half-carried, half-dragged her from the room while she remained frozen in place, eyes locked forward, unable even to look away.","No one protested. No one so much as whispered. Three hundred hardened journalists sat in absolute, obedient silence, and in that silence the room finally understood — not as a rumor, not as a headline, but as a bone-deep, physical fact — exactly what kind of woman had just walked off Dragon’s Grave without losing a single soldier.","Kaia leaned toward the microphone one final time. Her voice, when it came, had returned to its earlier, flat calm, as though the last ten seconds had never happened at all.","\"This conference is over.\"","She turned and left the stage without a backward glance.","---","Backstage, the corridor was quieter, lit by the low blue hum of maintenance strips embedded in the stone. Eros Strombane was already waiting against the wall, hands loose in his pockets, wearing the same easy, insufferable posture he always wore whenever he wanted to be the first thing someone saw. Co-leader of Eclipse. Son of Duke Strombane. And, at this precise moment, the very last person on Titan that Kaia wanted to look at.","\"Well,\" he said, pushing off the wall as she passed, \"the great Kaia Blackthorn just froze half a press corps solid over one little question. Your brother’s situation really does get under that ice-cold skin of yours, doesn’t it?\"","She kept walking. He fell into step beside her without being asked, the way he always did, like a shadow that had never once considered whether it was wanted.","\"My sister was in his year, you know,\" he went on, conversational, almost fond. \"I keep hoping his incompetence didn’t rub off on her.\" He drew a slim holographic phone from his pocket and flicked it open with a practiced motion. A shimmering, waist-high projection of a young woman with the same sharp Strombane features bloomed above his palm. He held it up like a trophy. \"Look at her. One of the fastest first-realm clears on record. Made it look easy.\" He leaned in and pressed a light, reverent kiss against the glowing surface of the image, eyes half-closing in a way that made even the hallway lighting seem to recoil.","Kaia stopped walking.","She turned, closed one hand around the projection with a grip that could have crushed diamond, and crushed it — not the delicate light of the hologram alone, but the phone itself, mana flaring white-hot for a fraction of a second as metal and enchanted glass folded inward like paper. The light died with a sharp, final crackle.","\"Shut up,\" she said, \"you sister-obsessed freak.\"","Eros pouted, an expression that on anyone else might have looked almost boyish. On him, it never quite reached his eyes — eyes that watched her the way a collector watches something rare and violent he hasn’t yet decided whether to cage or set loose.","\"So protective,\" he said softly. \"You really do love that brother of yours. It’s almost sweet. You’re hurting my feelings, you know.\"","She stepped in close — closer than she’d stood to anyone all day — and caught his jaw in one hand. The thin current of mana she’d kept leashed through the entire conference broke free again, sharper now, colder, close enough to him that frost began crawling along the collar of his uniform in delicate, spreading branches. The corridor’s temperature plummeted a second time in the space of ten minutes.","\"Never,\" she said, voice dropping into something far colder than anything the press room had heard, \"say that word near me again.\"","She held his gaze, unblinking, mana coiling visibly around her knuckles now, bright and dangerous. \"I hate that bastard.\"","The words did not sound like grief wearing anger’s mask. They sounded absolute. Final. A verdict handed down and sealed.","She released his jaw and turned away, already walking again before he’d fully recovered his footing.","\"You should be glad he came out classless,\" she added, not bothering to look back, her voice already flattening back into its practiced, public calm. \"Otherwise that bastard would have taken that record too.\"","She kept walking, boots striking the stone in the same precise, unhurried rhythm she’d used to cross the stage. Eros remained where he was, one hand absently touching the frost-rimed collar of his uniform, watching her disappear around the corner.","A slow smile spread across his face — not the easy, public one he wore for cameras and courtiers, but the other one. The private one. The one that only ever surfaced when something had genuinely, dangerously interested him.","The sheer, bottomless hate Kaia Blackthorn carried for her own brother was, in its own twisted way, magnificent.","And Eros Strombane had always had a weakness for magnificent things.","---"]},{"number":15,"title":"Chapter 15: What Neither of Them Says","paragraphs":["Lila woke to the low, steady sound of breathing beside her. For a moment the cave ceiling swam above her, unfamiliar and close. Then the weight of a hand registered — warm fingers loosely closed around hers. Rael was still asleep, body turned slightly toward her, face relaxed in a way she had not seen since the when he was unconscious before.","Her pulse jumped. She stayed very still and studied him. Even with the dirt and the fresh scratches across one cheek, the lines of his face were clean, almost severe when he was awake, softer now. His clothes were stiff with dried blood. Most of it, she realized, was not his own.","Memory returned in pieces. The ape’s hand around her skull. The pressure. The certain knowledge that her neck was about to give. Then the explosion behind the creature’s head and the sudden, heavy silence. She had been unconscious for the finishing blow, but the logic was simple. A newly awakened boy had killed a Savage-rank beast.","\"You’re full of surprises,\" she whispered.","She flexed her arms. No pain. The bones that had been broken felt whole. The cuts on her face were gone. He had used the only health potion on her. He could have kept it. He had been limping, bleeding, barely upright, and he had still poured the entire vial between her lips first.","Humans were selfish. That was the oldest rule she knew. Rael kept breaking it. He had caught her on the slope when falling would have been easier. He had stayed when the ape turned its full attention on him. He had carried her back on a leg that could barely take his own weight. Now he slept with one hand still holding hers so she would not wake alone.","She shifted closer. The space between them narrowed until she could feel the faint warmth of his breath. Her thoughts blurred. She found herself looking at his mouth without deciding to. The impulse rose — small, sharp, dangerous.","His eyes opened.","\"Lila. You’re up.\" The smile that followed was unguarded, genuine relief. No performance in it.","She pulled back at once, smoothing the moment away before it could settle. Later, she told herself. Maybe later.","\"Yes. Just now. Thank you for saving me.\"","\"I should be saying that to you.\" His voice was still rough from sleep. \"I’m glad it’s you who is stuck with me.\"","Heat touched her cheeks, and she busied her hands with the edge of her sleeve so she wouldn’t have to do anything with her face. If he noticed — and some small, embarrassed part of her assumed he must — he gave no sign of it. He simply looked at her the way he looked at everything, calm and unreadable, and let the silence pass without comment.","What she did not know — could not know — was that Rael had already read her perfectly. He had seen it days ago, in the way she positioned herself just slightly closer than the situation required, in the way her eyes lingered half a second too long before she remembered to look away. He was not dense. He had simply decided, somewhere in the space of a single held breath, to let the moment die quietly rather than name it. Better she believe he hadn’t noticed. Easier for both of them if the question never got asked aloud, because he did not yet know what answer he would give if it was.","He tried to sit up. Pain rolled through him in a single heavy wave. A short, muffled sound escaped before he could stop it.","Lila was at his side instantly, hands hovering like she wasn’t sure where it was safe to touch. \"What happened? Are you all right? Is it your leg? Your ribs?\"","\"I’m fine.\" He breathed through it, waiting for the wave to pass. \"Body’s just done. Adrenaline’s the only reason I got you back here at all. Now that it’s worn off, everything’s collecting its bill at once.\"","\"You shouldn’t have pushed through it. You should have told me you were that hurt.\"","\"And done what? Stopped halfway up a cliff to complain?\" A faint, tired smile. \"Wasn’t exactly a good moment for it.\"","She didn’t smile back. Her jaw tightened instead, some private frustration working behind her eyes.","\"Never mind. Sit still. Let me help you.\"","She helped him settle against the wall, one hand steady under his shoulder longer than strictly necessary.","\"If Suzy was here,\" she said, quieter now, \"she could have fixed the worst of it in seconds. Healing magic instead of me guessing with medicine and prayers.\"","\"Yeah.\" Rael’s voice dropped, some of the earlier warmth draining out of it. \"I don’t know if they’re even alive.\"","\"Alex is strong. He should be.\" She said it fast, like speed alone could make it true.","Rael nodded slowly, turning the thought over. \"In the short time we had him, he never once looked for a way out of anything. Kept his feet even when the ground didn’t want him to.\" A pause. \"That kind of stubbornness keeps people breathing longer than it should.\"","\"And Suzy?\"","\"If anyone protected her instinct to stay close to the group, it’s probably kept her alive.\"","Lila hugged her knees, some of the earlier tension bleeding out of her shoulders. \"I hope you’re right.\"","\"I hope I am too.\"","A silence settled, not uncomfortable, just full — the particular quiet of two people who had already run out of easy things to say and weren’t ready yet for the hard ones.","\"We rest,\" Rael said finally. \"When we can both move properly, we can go look for them.\"","\"It’s dangerous.\" Lila’s voice sharpened again, worry doing what embarrassment couldn’t. \"One Savage-rank Ape nearly ended us both. If there’s worse out there —\"","\"There is worse out there. That’s not a reason to stay in a hole until we starve.\" He flexed his fingers, testing the new density the level-ups had left behind, watching the motion like he was still getting used to owning it. \"It’ll be different this time. I’m stronger than before that fight. I’ll be stronger still by the time we’re ready to move.\"","He met her eyes, steady, certain in a way that didn’t ask for her agreement, only her trust. \"I don’t plan on losing anyone else. Not you. Not them, if we can help it.\"","Something in her chest loosened at that, though she didn’t examine it too closely. \"You say things like that so plainly. Like it’s obvious.\"","\"It is obvious. To me, at least.\"","She looked at him a moment longer than the conversation required, then busied herself with the storage ring before he could see whatever was sitting on her face.","She took out the horned-rabbit carcass and worked it with the goblin blade, efficient and quiet, hands moving with more confidence than her voice had a minute ago. This was the first real cooking either of them had done since entering the realm. Until now they had survived on the cold rations she had carried in. The smell of roasting meat filled the small space, rich and unfamiliar, curling up toward the low stone ceiling.","Rael cursed himself silently, watching her work. If he had known he would awaken, he would have packed properly — tools, better weapons, spare potions, dried supplies that didn’t rely on someone else’s foresight. The Emberwood wand was gone now too, destroyed in the ape’s neck, one more piece of borrowed convenience he’d have to replace with nothing but his own two hands.","\"You keep doing that,\" Lila said without looking up from the meat.","\"Doing what?\"","\"Going quiet and disappearing somewhere behind your own eyes. What are you thinking about?\"","He considered lying. Decided against it. \"How unprepared I was. How much easier this would be if I’d walked in with even half the equipment everyone else had.\"","\"You walked in with nothing and you’re already stronger than the rest of us combined.\" She glanced up at him, something almost teasing in it, an attempt to pull him back out of whatever dark corner he’d wandered into. \"I think you’re being too hard on yourself.\"","\"Or exactly hard enough. The realm doesn’t care about excuses. Being underprepared gets people killed just as easily as being weak.\"","\"You’re not weak. And you’re not as unprepared as you think. You have me.\" The words left her before she’d fully decided to say them, and she flushed again, quickly adding, \"And my supplies. My arrows. That’s what I meant.\"","\"I know what you meant,\" he said, and something in his tone — gentle, deliberately careless — let the moment pass without either of them having to stand inside it.","She handed him a portion of the rabbit, glad for the excuse to change the subject. The meat was denser than anything from Titan, the flavor clean and slightly wild, faintly smoky from the fire.","\"It’s better than normal rabbit meat,\" she said, chewing thoughtfully.","\"Higher mana density in the muscle, probably. Everything here runs richer than back home.\" He took another bite, savoring it despite himself, despite everything.","A soft chime sounded at the edge of his vision.","[+2 Agility]","He froze mid-chew.","Stats from food. He had never heard of it — not once, not in any book, not from any survivor’s account, not even whispered as rumor at his family’s dinner table. Back home the family had served high-level monster meat regularly, prepared by chefs who charged more for a single cut than most citizens earned in a year; the only benefit anyone had ever described was better taste, denser nutrition, faster physical recovery. No one gained attributes from eating. His family shared the Sovereign bloodline, even if their multipliers were a fraction of his. Nothing like this had ever been mentioned, not once, in sixteen years of listening to his siblings brag about every advantage they’d ever squeezed out of their power.","\"What is it?\" Lila asked, catching the stillness in his face.","\"Nothing.\" He turned the thought over carefully, chewing slower now, testing each bite like it might reveal something else but nothing was happening.","He turned the possibility over carefully. The class. The bloodline. Both of them felt wrong in the best possible way—unrestricted, as if the usual ceilings simply did not apply. He could keep growing for as long as he stayed alive. That was the entire condition.","He thought of the slope, the frost wolf, the ape’s foot coming down, the dozen smaller moments when death had been one bad angle away. Luck had carried him this far. Luck was not a strategy.","He finished the meat in silence, the new points settling into his legs like a quiet promise. Outside the blocked entrance the forest continued its patient work. Somewhere beyond the trees the summoner still directed the unnatural tide of high-level beasts.","Survive first. Grow after.","---"]},{"number":16,"title":"Chapter 16: Wild Boars","paragraphs":["Night had already settled over the forest when they left the cave. The moon hung thin and pale between the branches, casting just enough light to turn the ground into shifting patches of silver and black. Rael wanted Lila to take most of the kills. He sat at Level 9, close enough to the threshold that the exit gate might open for him at any moment. If he stepped through alone she would be left behind with almost no chance of surviving the rest of the First Realm. So he hung back whenever he could, letting her claim the finishing shots.","They moved in silence for hours. The woods felt wrong. No night birds called. No insects stirred. Even the wind seemed to hold its breath between the trunks. Both of them noticed the quiet, but nothing attacked and nothing revealed itself, so they kept walking. By the time the moon stood high the trees thinned a little and the ground grew softer underfoot.","That was when they found the boars.","Five of them rooted among the undergrowth, heavy bodies the color of wet earth. Levels eight and nine. One female stood slightly apart, larger than the others, her flanks thick with muscle. The four males formed a loose ring around her, heads low, tusks yellow and curved in the moonlight.","Rael decided this was a chance to test his body instead of his spells. The goblin blade in his hand was already scarred and edged with rust; it had never been meant for this much work. He slipped out of the brush and drove the blade upward into the female’s belly before she could turn. The impact jarred his arm to the shoulder. The boar screamed and twisted, the force of her thrashing ripping the blade free of his grip and flinging him sideways through the leaves. He hit the ground hard, rolled, and came up on his feet, lungs burning.","All five animals fixed on him at once. The males lowered their heads. The female staggered but stayed upright, blood pouring black in the moonlight.","\"Lila, now!\"","The arrow left the string with a soft hiss. A thin white line of light trailed behind it, bright against the dark trees, as if the shaft had cut a path through the night itself. It took the female clean through the eye. She dropped without another sound. The four surviving boars stared at the body for a single stunned second, then rage flooded their eyes. They charged.","Rael leapt for the nearest low branch, caught it, and used his legs to push off the trunk, launching himself farther than the jump alone would have carried him. One boar slammed into the tree where he had been a heartbeat earlier, wood cracking under the impact. Rael hit the ground in a roll that brought him right beside the dead female. He yanked the blade free of her body just as the remaining three closed in again.","He shouted \"Ignite!\" and threw himself sideways. Fire erupted under the lead boar’s hooves, orange and violent against the dark. Flames climbed its legs and belly. While it screamed and tried to put itself out, Lila’s next arrow punched through its neck, another white trail cutting the night. The animal collapsed, still burning.","Three left.","They came at him in a tight wedge, tusks lowered, eyes black with fury. Rael did not meet them head-on. He sidestepped the first rush, letting the middle boar’s momentum carry it past, then dropped low and swung the blade in a short, vicious arc across the rear legs of the animal on his right. The edge bit deep into tendon. The boar’s hindquarters collapsed sideways and it crashed to the ground with a wet, furious squeal. Before it could scramble up, Rael was already moving. He drove the tip of the blade straight into the soft socket of its nearest eye. The creature convulsed once and went still.","Two remaining.","The last pair wheeled faster than he expected. One clipped his thigh with a tusk as it passed; cloth tore and blood welled, warm against the cool night air. He staggered, recovered, and forced his breathing steady. Lila’s bowstring sang again. The arrow streaked forward, leaving that same thin white line behind it, and buried itself in the shoulder of the closer boar, knocking it off stride. That was all the opening Rael needed. He closed the distance in three long strides, dropped under the animal’s snap of tusks, and slashed both front legs at the joint. The boar’s weight betrayed it. It pitched forward onto its chest. Rael stepped onto its back, drove the blade down through the base of the skull, and twisted. Bone cracked. The body went limp.","One left.","The final boar did not charge. It circled, foam dripping from its mouth, eyes never leaving Rael’s face. Lila moved through the trees, silent, but this time she did not stay at range. She slipped closer, step by careful step, until she was almost within the animal’s reach. The boar feinted left, then lunged right, trying to catch Rael between its tusks and a thick oak. He rolled under the attack, came up on one knee, and stabbed upward into the soft flesh beneath the jaw. The blade sank deep but did not find the brain. The boar reared, ripping the weapon from his hands and hurling him backward into the roots of the oak.","Pain flared along his ribs. He tasted blood. The boar spun, ready to finish him.","Lila stepped out of the shadows at its flank. She was close enough that the animal’s heat washed over her. Instead of loosing from afar she drew the string to her cheek, waited for the exact moment the boar’s head swung toward Rael, and released. The arrow left a bright white trail through the dark and drove straight into the soft tissue behind the ear. The animal staggered, bellowed once, and collapsed three steps from Rael’s outstretched hand.","Silence returned to the clearing, heavier than before.","Rael lay still for several breaths, staring at the canopy of black leaves. Every muscle burned. The cut on his thigh pulsed. He pushed himself upright anyway, retrieved the goblin blade, and wiped it clean on the nearest carcass. Lila stepped fully into the moonlight, bow still in hand, eyes scanning the surrounding woods.","\"You took too many risks,\" she said.","\"Had to feel the weight of them,\" he answered. His voice came out rough. He glanced down at his hands, still trembling slightly from the effort. \"Looks like my body’s still not recovered enough. That’s why I’m struggling in hand-to-hand combat with these same-level beasts.\"","They worked quickly after that, stripping what meat they could use and storing the rest in Lila’s ring. The forest remained quiet. No other beasts came to investigate the blood. That silence pressed on both of them like a hand on the back of the neck.","By the time they started back toward the cave the moon had begun to sink. Rael walked with a slight limp, the blade hanging loose at his side. Lila stayed a few paces behind, arrows ready. Neither spoke of the empty woods or the way every sound seemed to stop the moment they entered a new stretch of trees. They simply kept moving, step after careful step, while the night held its breath around them.","---"]},{"number":17,"title":"Chapter 17: Ape [1]","paragraphs":["They reached the cave just as the sky began to lighten. The last of the night still clung to the trees, but the air already carried the first hint of morning. Rael and Lila set the stored meat inside, then sat against opposite walls of the narrow space. He checked his status while the quiet settled around them.","```","STATUS","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Copied Classes: Flamecaller (C-Rank), Archer (B-Rank)","Level: 9","Souls: 568 → 588","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Strength: 36","Agility: 42","Mana: 40","Vitality: 36","Additional Stats: 8","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert), Eagle Eyes (Expert), Piercing Arrow (Expert)","```","The souls had climbed a little from the boars. Nothing dramatic. He closed the window and looked across at Lila.","\"How much level did you reach?\" he asked.","\"5,\" she said. \"What about you?\"","\"7.\"","He left it at that. The multiplier stayed his secret which he didn’t wanted to tell anyone until he have full trust on them. She didn’t press. They talked a little longer about nothing important—the way the meat had cooked the night before, the strange silence in the woods—then Lila started preparing breakfast. Boar meat sizzled over a small fire she built near the entrance. The smell filled the cave. By the time they finished eating, the sun had climbed high enough that the light outside turned sharp and clear. Afternoon heat pressed against the stone.","That was when the scream came.","It was a girl’s voice, high and raw with terror, and it was getting closer. Rael and Lila locked eyes for half a second, then both moved. They slipped out of the cave and started down the slope. Even at a distance Eagle Eyes let them see the clearing below with painful clarity.","Suzy stood near the edge of the trees, frozen. Two others fought beside her. A young man with a long, two-handed sword held the center, feet planted wide. A girl in light blue robes—water mage, from the way the air shimmered around her hands—stood a few paces to his left. Between them and the tree line a giant ape smashed the ground with both fists. The creature stood nearly twice the height of a man, thick gray fur matted with dirt, eyes small and furious. Its arms looked long enough to reach the swordsman from three strides away.","Lila already had an arrow on the string. Rael started running.","The water mage struck first. A dense jet of water slammed into the ape’s left leg, hard enough to make the knee buckle for a moment. The ape roared and swung both arms in wide, crushing arcs. The swordsman raised his blade and caught the first blow on the flat. The impact drove him back two full steps, boots carving grooves in the dirt. He staggered, recovered, and held the line.","Lila’s arrow streaked across the open ground, a thin white line of light cutting the afternoon air. It buried itself in the back of the ape’s right hand. The creature jerked its arm up and snarled, more annoyed than hurt. The three people in the clearing glanced toward the mountain for a fraction of a second, realizing help had arrived, then the fight swallowed their attention again.","The swordsman charged. He used the slope of the ground to add speed, sword sweeping in a full horizontal circle. Even when the blade missed clean flesh the force of the swing forced the ape to lean away. The edge finally connected across the ribs and only scraped a shallow white line through the thick fur. The ape answered with a backhand that the swordsman barely managed to parry. Metal rang. The young man’s arms shook.","The water mage raised both hands. A curved wall of water rose between the ape and the swordsman just as the next fist came down. The impact hit the barrier and slowed. Force bled away into spray and mist. The ape’s knuckles still reached the swordsman, but the blow landed heavy rather than lethal. The young man staggered, coughed, and kept his feet.","Lila loosed again. Another white trail. This time the arrow took the ape high on the shoulder. It barely flinched. She was already nocking the next shaft.","Rael kept running. The distance was longer than it looked. Roots and uneven ground slowed him. He could hear the ape’s breathing now—deep, wet, angry—and the sharp ring of steel each time the long sword met those massive arms.","The swordsman tried a different approach. He dropped his center of gravity, let the ape’s next swing pass over his head, and cut upward in a rising slash aimed at the underside of the arm. The blade bit deeper this time. Dark blood welled and ran down the fur. The ape bellowed and slammed both fists into the earth. The shockwave knocked the swordsman off balance. He rolled, came up on one knee, and barely raised the sword in time to catch the follow-up strike. The force drove him to one knee. His arms trembled under the weight.","The water mage sent another concentrated blast, this one aimed at the ape’s face. Water exploded across its eyes and snout. For a moment the creature recoiled, shaking its head, blinded by the sudden flood. The swordsman used the opening. He surged forward and drove the tip of the long sword toward the soft tissue under the jaw. The ape twisted at the last instant. The blade scored a deep cut along the neck but missed the artery. Blood sprayed, hot and dark, and the ape’s roar turned into something closer to a scream of pure rage.","Lila’s third arrow arrived in that same second, white light trailing behind it, and sank into the already wounded shoulder. The ape’s head snapped toward the mountain. Its small eyes found the distant figure of the archer. Then its gaze dropped to the water mage still standing in the open, hands raised for another spell.","The creature’s entire body shifted. It stopped focusing on the swordsman. It planted both feet, lowered its head, and charged straight at the girl in blue.","Rael was still too far away.","---"]},{"number":18,"title":"Chapter 18: Ape [2]","paragraphs":["The ape charged in blind rage showing his large sharp teeths.","The water mage Nathia’s hands snapped up. Water erupted from the ground in thick, coiling bands that wrapped around the creature’s legs and torso—Water Shackle. The spell should have rooted anything of ordinary size. Against the giant ape it barely slowed the rush. The bands stretched, strained, and began to tear almost at once. The ape’s bulk carried it forward like a falling tree.","An arrow cut the air with a thin white trail of light. It sank deep into the side of the ape’s neck. The impact jerked the creature’s head sideways just enough that the swinging arm passed over Nathia instead of through her. She dropped to one knee, heart hammering so hard she could hear it in her ears. Another half-second and she would have been paste on the dirt.","Luke the Swordsmen was already moving. He sprinted in from the side, long sword flashing in a low arc aimed at the ape’s ankle. The blade left a distorted ribbon of wind in its wake, the air itself seeming to part under the force of the swing. \"As if I’ll let you kill her!\" he shouted. The edge bit into thick tendon and drew a dark spray of blood. The ape roared, more furious than hurt, and swung its free arm in a wild backhand that forced Luke to leap clear.","Nathia scrambled backward, still on her feet but shaking. Suzy stood only a short distance away, eyes wide, hands pressed to her mouth. The ape’s small, hateful eyes found her. It shifted its weight onto its uninjured foot and lunged.","Suzy screamed.","Luke threw himself into the path without hesitation. He planted both feet, raised the long sword horizontally, and took the full force of the charge on the flat of the blade. The impact drove him backward several paces, boots carving deep furrows in the earth, but he held. For a moment it looked as if he might actually stop the creature.","The ape’s mouth pulled into something that almost resembled a smile.","Both massive arms came together, fists locked, and the ape pulled them back like a battering ram. Then it drove the combined weight straight into Luke’s right arm. Bone cracked with a sound like green wood splitting. The long sword flew from his grip and spun across the clearing. Luke’s face went white. His arm hung at a wrong angle, useless.","\"Luke!\" Nathia’s voice broke. Tears already streaked her cheeks. She slammed another Water Shackle around the ape’s legs and followed it with a concentrated Water Blast. The jet of pressurized water hammered the creature’s chest. At the same moment one of Lila’s arrows streaked in from the mountain, white trail bright in the afternoon light. The arrow and the water jet crossed paths. The shaft lost most of its force and only scraped fur. The blast itself peeled a layer of skin from the ape’s torso but failed to drive it back.","Luke staggered, still trying to stay between the beast and the two girls. \"Nathia—take Suzy and run!\"","The ape ignored the order. It reached down with one enormous hand, closed around Luke’s hips, and lifted him clear of the ground as easily as a child lifts a doll. The long sword lay too far away to matter now. Luke kicked and struggled with his one good arm. The ape’s grip tightened.","Luke’s eyes found Nathia. Panic and something like apology mixed in them. As if the end hit him suddenly. He was ready to die for them but when the death was just about to come he broke.","\"Nath... Nathia... save me... me... I don’t want to die—\"","The words cut off in a wet, crunching sound. The ape’s hand closed fully. Luke’s lower body simply came apart. Legs and hips dropped to the dirt in a spreading red mess. The upper half remained in the ape’s fist, still twitching. Nathia’s scream tore the air. Suzy’s hands covered her face, but her eyes stayed open, fixed on the horror.","The ape brought the remains closer to its mouth and bit down. Bone cracked. Flesh tore. It chewed with slow, almost thoughtful motions, blood running in thick ropes from its jaws, as if the young man were nothing more than a piece of soft fruit. When it finished it opened its hand and let the ruined torso fall. The body hit the ground with a dull, final sound.","Nathia dropped to her knees. Sobs shook her entire frame. \"No... no, no, no...\" Suzy stood frozen a few paces away, face bloodless, staring at what was left of Luke.","The ape turned its head toward the two remaining girls.","A white-trailed arrow took it full in the right eye.","The creature staggered backward, roaring, one massive hand clamped over the ruined socket. Balance failed. It stumbled two steps and nearly went down.","That was when Rael arrived.","He came sliding down the last stretch of slope in a controlled skid, dirt and leaves flying. He hit the flat ground running, scooped Luke’s fallen long sword up with one hand, and never slowed. The blade felt heavy but usable; his Strength was high enough now to swing it without trouble.","Suzy’s head snapped toward him. For a fraction of a second something unreadable flickered behind her wide, terrified eyes—then pure relief flooded her face. \"Rael!\" she cried, voice cracking.","Nathia looked up through her tears, saw the newcomer, and could only stare, still on her knees, still shaking.","Rael did not answer either of them. He focused on the ape. The skill activated the moment the sword left his hand.","\"Piercing Arrow.\"","The long blade became a streak of force. It punched straight through the ape’s stomach and exited the far side in a spray of dark blood and ruined flesh. The creature’s roar turned into a high, broken scream. Rael never stopped moving. He reached the still-standing body, used the protruding sword hilt as a foothold, and launched himself upward. His legs locked around the thick neck. Both hands clamped onto the opposite sides of the ape’s head, palms pressed hard against the ears and temples.","\"Ignite.\"","Flame exploded between his hands and the ape’s skull. The heat seared his own palms instantly, skin blistering, but he did not let go. The ape thrashed, nails raking deep furrows down Rael’s back. Pain flared white-hot across his shoulders. He only tightened his grip.","\"Ignite.\"","More fire. The smell of burning fur and cooking flesh filled the clearing. The ape’s remaining eye rolled wildly. It spun, slammed itself against trees, tried everything to dislodge the human locked onto its head. Rael’s burned hands stayed locked in place.","\"Ignite. Ignite. Ignite—\"","Each word sent another surge of flame into the already ruined skull. The ape’s struggles grew weaker. Its roar faded into wet, bubbling sounds. Finally the massive body swayed, then collapsed forward like a felled tower. Rael rode it all the way down. When the impact came he was thrown clear, rolling across the dirt until he came to a stop on his back.","Both of his hands were blackened and raw. Blood ran freely from the deep scratches along his spine. The ape lay motionless, head a charred, half-melted ruin.","Nathia stared at him as if she could not decide whether he was real. Suzy had both hands pressed to her mouth again, eyes shining with what looked like pure, stunned gratitude. Neither of them spoke. The only sound left in the clearing was the soft crackle of residual flame and the ragged breathing of the three survivors.","Rael pushed himself up to a sitting position, hands throbbing, and looked at what remained of Luke. Then he looked at the two girls who were still alive.","The fight was over.","For now.","---"]},{"number":19,"title":"Chapter 19: Childhood Friend","paragraphs":["The notifications flickered across Rael’s vision the moment the ape’s body finished settling into the dirt.","[+ Level up]","[+ Level up]","[+ Level up]","He ignored them. His hands were still locked in a half-open position, blackened and raw from the repeated Ignite. Every small movement sent fresh waves of pain up his arms. He stayed on his knees, breathing hard, staring at the ruined corpse of the creature and the smaller, more terrible remains a short distance away.","Suzy reached him first.","She ran the last few steps and dropped to her knees in front of him, arms wrapping around his shoulders before he could react. Her face pressed against the side of his neck. Hot tears soaked into his collar almost at once.","\"Rael...\" Her voice cracked on the name. \"Rael, you’re alive.\"","He could feel the soft press of her chest against him. Heat rose in his face for a reason that had nothing to do with the burns. He did not push her away. He understood the weight of what she had just watched. In her mind he had been dead for days, and now he had appeared out of nowhere and dragged her back from the edge.","\"I’m here,\" he said quietly. \"You’re alive too, Suzy.\"","He tried to return the embrace, but the moment his damaged hands touched her back the pain forced a sharp breath out of him. He settled for letting her hold on while he stayed still.","Lila stepped out of the trees a few seconds later, bow still in hand. She saw the two of them locked together and stopped. Something tight moved through her chest. She remembered the first day in the realm, the way Suzy had looked at Rael and spoken her interest without shame. Lila had told herself those feelings did not matter, that survival came first. Yet the sight of Suzy’s arms around him still settled in a quiet corner of her mind and refused to leave. She said nothing. Her eyes moved past them and landed on the other girl.","Nathia.","Recognition hit like a physical blow. Lila had not looked closely at the water mage’s face during the fight. Now, with the danger gone, she saw the familiar features clearly—the same girl she had known since childhood, the same quiet intensity that had always set Nathia apart. Lila’s breath caught.","Nathia was no longer looking at anyone living. She crawled on hands and knees toward what was left of Luke. Her voice came out broken, barely more than a whisper at first.","\"Luke... Luke...\"","Then the sound rose into a raw, open cry that seemed to tear something inside her throat.","\"Aaaah—Luke—\"","She reached the scattered remains and laid both hands on the nearest piece of him, the lower half that still wore the torn remnants of his trousers. Her shoulders shook so hard it looked like she might come apart. Tears fell in thick streams, mixing with the blood on the ground.","The memories came without mercy.","They had studied in the same school for years. Nathia had watched Luke from the edge of every classroom, every training yard. He never seemed to notice the way her eyes followed him. She never found the courage to speak. When the transfer to the Dream Realm came, fear finally pushed her past silence. She had walked up to him in the chaos of the first hours and asked if they could team up. Luke had looked surprised, then simply nodded and said yes. In the days that followed he had protected her without hesitation—blocking blows meant for her, pulling her out of the path of claws, sharing what little food they found. One night, while they sat against a tree listening to distant howls, the words had finally left her mouth. She told him she had liked him for a long time. That she was glad they had ended up together here. Luke had gone quiet for a long moment. Then he reached over and rested his hand over hers.","\"When we get back,\" he had said, voice low and steady, \"I’ll give you a proper answer. Not here. Not while everything is trying to kill us. Wait for me until then.\"","She had believed him. She had held on to that promise through every fight since.","Now the promise lay in pieces on the dirt.","Lila crossed the clearing and sank down beside her. Tears already blurred her own vision.","\"Nathia,\" she said, the name coming out soft and broken.","Nathia turned her head slowly. When she saw Lila’s face the last of her control shattered. A long, keening sob tore out of her. She fell forward against Lila’s shoulder and clung to her like a child.","\"Lila... Lila... Luke died... he just... he just died...\"","\"I’m so sorry.\" Lila’s voice cracked. She wrapped both arms around her friend and rocked her gently. \"I’m so sorry, dear. So sorry.\"","The words felt useless. In the back of Lila’s mind a colder thought kept circling: if her arrow had not crossed paths with the water blast, if it had struck cleanly a second earlier, Luke might have had the time to pull free. The knowledge sat like a stone in her chest. She kept murmuring soft, broken comforts while Nathia’s cries rose and fell against her.","Rael watched them for a moment longer, then spoke quietly to Suzy.","\"Suzy. Let’s go to them.\"","She nodded, wiped her face with the back of one hand, and slipped her shoulder under his arm to help him stand. The movement sent fresh fire through his burned palms, but he forced himself upright. Together they crossed the short distance.","He stood over the two girls, Suzy still supporting most of his weight.","\"Lila,\" he said. His voice was rough but steady. \"We have to go. We can’t stay out here.\"","He meant the words. Sympathy for Nathia sat heavy in him, but daylight made every open space a risk. Even with the strength he had gained, a creature like the Frost Dire Wolf could still end them all. Remaining in the open was a slow form of suicide.","Lila looked up, eyes red. She spoke gently to Nathia.","\"We have to hide. Nathia, we can’t stay here.\"","Nathia shook her head at first, still clutching the bloodied ground. Lila kept talking, soft and persistent, until the other girl finally let herself be drawn to her feet. The two of them began to move, Nathia leaning heavily against Lila, still crying in long, broken waves.","Rael turned toward what remained of Luke. He raised one ruined hand.","\"I’m sorry if only I have come sooner.\" His expression remain neutral even though he felt regret.","\"Fireball.\"","The spell formed smaller than usual—his control was poor through the pain—but the flame caught and spread. The body burned. The smell of it mixed with the already heavy scent of the ape’s corpse. Rael’s arms trembled from the effort. He did not look away until the fire had done its work.","They started back toward the cave in a slow, uneven group. Lila guided Nathia. Suzy kept Rael upright. Nathia’s quiet sobbing never fully stopped; it rose and fell with every step, a raw sound that followed them through the trees. Once, when the path grew steeper, Suzy leaned in and pressed a soft kiss to Rael’s cheek.","\"Thank you for coming,\" she whispered.","\"No need,\" he answered. The words came out quieter than he intended.","They kept walking. The sun continued its slow slide toward the western treeline. Behind them the clearing grew smaller, the smoke from the two fires thinning into the afternoon sky. Ahead, the cave waited—narrow, dark, and for the moment still safe.","None of them spoke again for a long time. Only Nathia’s broken crying marked the distance they covered, a sound that seemed to belong to the forest itself now, as if the trees had decided to remember the dead.","---"]},{"number":20,"title":"Chapter 20: Never Apologise For Living","paragraphs":["They reached the cave as the last light was fading. Suzy guided Rael inside and eased him down against the cool stone wall. The space that had once felt just large enough for two now pressed in around four bodies. There was barely room to sit without touching someone else. Nathia slumped near the back, knees drawn up, eyes fixed on nothing. The quiet sobs that had followed them through the trees had finally stopped, but the emptiness in her face remained.","Rael flexed his burned hands carefully and looked at Suzy.","\"Can you heal these?\"","She knelt in front of him and took one of his wrists, turning the blackened skin toward the fading light. Her expression tightened.","\"No. They’re too deep. My healing won’t reach that far.\"","Rael started to pull his hand back, already thinking through other options, when Suzy reached into her storage ring and drew out a small glass vial filled with clear liquid that caught the last of the daylight.","\"But I have something better.\"","She held the health potion out to him. Rael stared at it for a long moment. He knew exactly how rare those were, how tightly the royal family controlled their supply, how many credits even a single vial could cost someone without noble backing.","\"You sure?\" he asked. \"That’s not something you just hand over.\"","Suzy closed both of her hands around his and pressed the vial into his palm. Her voice came out soft but steady.","\"I’m sure. You being at your best is good for all of us.\"","Lila watched from where she sat beside Nathia. She saw the way Suzy’s eyes lingered on Rael’s face, the quiet affection that had never really gone away since the first day they met. Something sharp and complicated moved through Lila’s chest. She wanted, for a brief second, to stand up and say something of her own. Instead she turned back to Nathia, resting a hand on her friend’s shoulder and saying nothing.","Rael uncorked the vial and drank. Light washed over him in a soft, steady wave. The blackened skin on his hands cracked and fell away in thin flakes. New flesh formed beneath it, pink at first, then settling into the same pale tone as the rest of his arms. The deep scratches along his back closed. The older scars on his face from the fall down the slope faded until only smooth skin remained. Within fifteen minutes he felt whole again, as if the burns and the raking nails had never happened.","He turned his hands over, testing the fingers.","\"These potions always surprise me,\" he said. \"How does the royal family even make them?\"","Suzy sat down beside him, close enough that their shoulders touched. She took one of his newly healed hands in both of hers and held it.","\"I don’t know. I’ve never understood it either.\" She was quiet for a moment, then added, \"I thought both of you died that day.\"","\"We thought the same about you,\" Rael answered. He hesitated, then asked the question that had been sitting at the edge of his mind since they found her. \"What about Alex?\"","A single tear slipped down Suzy’s cheek. She didn’t wipe it away.","\"He blocked the path between me and the Frost Wolf. Told me to run. I... I ran. I’m so ashamed. He died because of me. It’s should have been me who should have died.\"","Rael looked at her for a long time. When he spoke, his voice was low and careful, as if he were saying the words to himself as much as to her.","\"It’s not your fault. No one should ever apologize for living.\"","Suzy’s grip on his hand tightened. She didn’t speak, but she didn’t let go either. Rael left the silence alone. After a while he glanced across the cave toward Lila. She met his eyes and gave a small nod. The decision passed between them without words.","\"We should rest for now,\" Rael said.","The three girls arranged themselves along the back wall, Nathia still quiet between Lila and Suzy. Rael settled near the entrance where he could watch the narrow opening. Once the others’ breathing had slowed, he finally opened his status.","```","STATUS","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Class: Copy (F-Rank)","Copied Classes: Flamecaller (C-Rank), Archer (B-Rank)","Level: 9 → 12","Souls: 588 → 846","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign (+100% Multiplier) (Dormant)","Strength: 36 → 42","Agility: 42 → 48","Mana: 40 → 46","Vitality: 36 → 42","Additional Stats: 6","Skills: Fireball (Apprentice), Ignite (Expert), Eagle Eyes (Expert), Piercing Arrow (Expert)","```","Three levels. The jump had pushed him well past the threshold. Sooner or later the gate would appear for him whether he wanted it or not. The realm did not allow people to linger once they crossed that line. If he wanted the others to survive after he was forced out, he would have to help them climb as fast as possible.","He focused on his main class.","[Class]","[Copy (F-Rank) → Analyze (E-Rank)]","[Souls Required: 846/1000]","One hundred and fifty-four souls short. Everything else could wait. Skills he copied would disappear when the month ended unless he made them permanent somehow, and the only path he could see toward that was pushing Copy higher. From now on every kill had to count. Every soul had to go toward the upgrade.","Outside the cave the night settled in fully. Inside, four people breathed in the narrow dark—one still broken by grief, one quietly lying by the others, one watching over a childhood friend, and one staring at the numbers that would decide how much longer he could stay.","---"]},{"number":21,"title":"Chapter 21: Knowing [1]","paragraphs":["Two days passed.","Nathia began to come back to herself in small, careful steps. She still did not laugh. She still did not smile. When someone spoke to her the answers came short and flat, but the hollow look in her eyes had started to ease. She ate when food was offered. She stood when it was time to move. Most of all, she had decided she would keep living. Rael understood that choice better than most. After the Blackthorn gates had closed behind him he had made the same quiet promise to himself: even if no one wanted him, he would stay alive until something finally took the choice away.","At night the four of them left the cave and hunted. Rael stayed at the edges of every fight. He no longer wanted levels of his own. Every kill that could go to the others did. He only stepped in when something looked ready to overwhelm them. Suzy climbed faster than the rest. Rael guessed it had to do with her class. Priestess was not built for direct combat, so the system seemed to reward her differently. Lila and Nathia both told him they had reached Level 7. He had no way to check, so he simply took their word.","On the fourth day the cave felt smaller than ever. Four people, their gear, the smell of old smoke and dried blood—it pressed in from every side. Rael sat near the entrance and spoke to Lila while the others rested.","\"We need more wood. And we need a bigger place. At the rate we’re going it’ll still take more than two weeks for everyone to hit ten.\"","Lila nodded. \"I know. This space is already too tight.\"","\"I’ll go look during the day,\" Rael said. \"We can’t search properly at night. Too easy to miss something, or walk into worse trouble.\"","Suzy sat up at once. \"I’ll come with you.\"","Lila’s shoulders tightened. Over the last two days Suzy had spent almost every free moment near Rael—sitting beside him while they ate, walking close when they moved, finding reasons to speak to him first. Lila had been the one staying with Nathia, the one listening to the quiet crying that still came at odd hours. She missed the earlier days when it had been only the two of them in the cave. She would never say it out loud, but the feeling sat there anyway.","\"Suzy,\" Lila said, keeping her voice even, \"I need you here. Nathia still isn’t steady. Someone has to help with the cooking and keep an eye on her.\"","Suzy blinked, then seemed to remember. \"Ah... right. You’re right.\" She looked between Lila and Rael, clearly disappointed. \"Then who goes with him? It’s dangerous alone.\"","Lila held her ground. \"Yeah. Who will.\"","Nathia stood up. The movement was slow but deliberate. \"I can go.\"","Both girls turned toward her. For a moment neither spoke. Rael broke the silence.","\"Yeah. She can come.\"","Suzy’s mouth opened, then closed again. \"Yes... go with her.\"","The decision settled. Rael and Nathia left the cave and started along the opposite face of the mountain, looking for any opening large enough to hold four people with room to spare. They found nothing that matched what they needed—shallow overhangs, cracks too narrow, one promising mouth that opened into a dead end after only a few steps. High-level beasts stayed out of sight the entire time. It was as if the Summoner had simply moved on, or lost interest in this stretch of forest.","They had been inside the First Realm for more than two weeks now. Time moved differently here. One day in the Elysian Wilds equaled two on Titan. By that measure Rael had been gone from the ordinary world for over a month. He found himself wondering what his small apartment in Bloomingdale smelled like. He had left dishes in the sink the night before the transfer. The thought pulled a quiet sigh out of him.","Nathia glanced over. \"What happened?\"","\"Nothing important. Just remembering Titan. Wondering what kind of state my room is in by now.\"","She studied him for a moment. \"I never thought you’d think about things like that. It’s actually a relief. You think like a normal person.\"","Rael raised an eyebrow. \"What did you think I was?\"","It was the first easy conversation they had managed since the day Luke died. Nathia’s mouth almost curved, though the expression never quite became a smile.","\"I thought you were some rich boy with a hundred maids cleaning up after him.\"","\"You’re wrong then.\"","They walked a little farther in silence before Rael asked, \"What about you and Lila? How do you know each other?\"","Nathia’s eyes softened as the older memories rose. She set Luke carefully to the side for a moment and answered.","\"All the children in Bloomingdale go to the same university. I figured you wouldn’t know that.\"","\"I didn’t.\"","\"Lila and I met a long time ago. Kindergarten. We were neighbors before...\" She paused, then finished quietly, \"before her parents passed away. She moved after that. We only saw each other at university later on. Bloomingdale isn’t a big town. Everyone knows everyone to some degree when you grow up there.\"","Rael nodded. \"Must be nice getting awakened near people you already know.\"","Nathia looked at him more carefully. \"How come I never saw you before? Trust me, I would have remembered a face like yours if I’d seen it even once.\"","\"I only moved there just before the awakening. Personal reasons. Sorry I can’t tell you.\"","\"Oh. Its okay.\" She seemed to file the information away. \"That explains it. You and Suzy were the only ones I didn’t already know.\"","Rael stopped walking.","The words landed with a strange weight. He turned his head toward her.","\"You didn’t know Suzy? I thought she was your friend. The way she looked at ease with others when I first saw her i thought she was friends with Lila and other’s... I assumed you three had history.\"","Nathia shook her head. \"No. I thought she was your friend. She seemed so happy the first time she saw you.\"","Rael stood still for several seconds. Something cold and quiet moved through the back of his mind. He had never met Suzy before the Dream Realm either. And in all the time since, through every injury and every fight, she had never once used a healing skill. Not on him. Not on anyone. A priestess who never healed.","He looked back the way they had come, toward the distant cave where Lila and Suzy waited.","\"Lila,\" he said under his breath.","The single name hung in the afternoon air.","---"]},{"number":22,"title":"Chapter 22: Knowing [2]","paragraphs":["The realization settled in Rael’s chest like a cold weight. He could not yet say with certainty that Suzy hid something dangerous, but the pieces refused to fit any other way. Nathia had never known her before the Dream Realm. Neither had he. And in all the days they had spent together—through injuries, fights, and quiet moments in the cave—Suzy had never once used a priestess skill. When his hands were burned she had simply offered a health potion. A real priestess would have tried to heal him herself.","He turned and started running back toward the cave.","Nathia caught up after a few strides, breath already short. \"Rael, what happened? Why are you suddenly running?\"","\"Run with me,\" he said. \"I have a bad feeling about this.\"","They covered the distance in silence after that, the only sound the steady impact of their boots against the earth.","Inside the cave, the two women who had stayed behind were preparing food. Lila knelt by the small fire, feeding it carefully with dry sticks. Suzy sat a short distance away, slicing strips of boar meat with a short knife. The ordinary sounds of the work—the soft crackle of flame, the rhythmic cut of the blade—filled the narrow space.","Suzy spoke without looking up. \"Hey. I’ve been meaning to ask. Why do you only hunt at night?\"","Lila kept her eyes on the fire. \"We never told you, did we?\"","\"Told me what?\"","\"Rael has a theory about why the high-level beasts are here in the First Realm.\" Lila added another stick to the flames. \"He thinks there’s a Summoner. Someone pulling the strong ones in from outside. That’s why we move at night—he believes the Summoner’s control weakens then.\"","The knife stopped mid-cut.","Suzy’s hands went still. The soft sound of metal against flesh vanished. Lila glanced over, frowning.","\"What’s wrong, Suzy?\"","A slow smile pulled at the corner of Suzy’s mouth. When she spoke, her voice was low, almost thoughtful.","\"Of course he knows. The Solaria genius.\"","Lila rose halfway to her feet. \"Why are you saying that?\"","Suzy turned her head. The change happened in the space of a single breath. Her teeth lengthened and sharpened. The soft lines of her face hardened, cheekbones sharpening, eyes darkening with something that was no longer human. Her hair shifted color, threads of deep red spreading through the strands like spilled ink. The air around her thickened with a sudden, heavy bloodlust that made the small cave feel colder.","Lila froze.","Suzy stood, the knife still loose in her fingers, and took one slow step toward her.","Rael and Nathia reached the cave mouth at a full run and stepped inside. The fire still burned low. The smell of cooking meat still hung in the air. But the two women were gone.","Blood marked the stone floor near the fire in a wide, uneven splash. Some of it had already begun to dry dark at the edges.","Rael sank down against the wall, staring at the stain. \"Shit. Shit.\"","Nathia moved closer, voice tight. \"Rael, what happened?\"","He did not look at her. \"It was Suzy. She’s the Summoner.\"","Nathia stared at him for several long seconds before the words fully landed. When they did, her face went pale. The pieces rearranged themselves in her mind with brutal clarity—the timing, the missing beasts, the way the ape had turned toward Suzy only for Luke to die protecting her.","Rael pushed himself back to his feet. \"She summoned those creatures. And now she’s taken Lila.\" He dragged a hand through his hair. \"I don’t understand why she waited this long. Why not attack earlier?\" The answer came almost as soon as he asked the question. \"Maybe Lila told her about the theory. Maybe that was the moment she decided she couldn’t risk us knowing.\"","Nathia’s hands curled into fists. \"No. This can’t be true.\" Then the memory of the clearing returned—the ape’s sudden shift toward Suzy, Luke throwing himself in the way. \"She... she let Luke die.\"","Rael was already moving toward the entrance. \"We have to search. They can’t have gone far.\"","They stepped back into the afternoon light. Rael activated Eagle Eyes and swept the surrounding forest in every direction. Nothing moved. No birds. No small animals. No sign of two human figures. The entire region felt emptied out, as if something had quietly erased every living presence except the two of them.","They searched through the rest of the afternoon. Every promising trail ended in bare earth. Every distant sound turned out to be only wind. By the time the light began to soften, Rael knew they would find nothing more that day.","He stopped and turned to Nathia. \"We have to find a new place to stay. She already knows this cave. Staying here is the same as waiting for her to come back.\"","Nathia’s eyes were bright with anger and the leftover grief that never fully left her. \"I’m going to kill that bitch.\"","Rael understood the fury. He felt a version of it himself. But standing still and feeding the rage would only get them killed.","\"Now isn’t the time,\" he said. \"We move first. Then we hunt her.\"","Nathia held his gaze for a long moment. Finally she gave a short, sharp nod. The two of them turned away from the bloodstained cave and started walking, searching for any new shelter the forest might still offer before night returned.","---"]},{"number":23,"title":"Chapter 23: Wolf","paragraphs":["Rael walked with his head down, cursing himself under his breath. Ever since he’d set foot in the First Realm, his thinking had gone soft, sloppy, forgivable only in hindsight. The signs had been there the whole time. Suzy never once using a priestess skill. The way high-level beasts appeared and vanished like they’d been summoned on cue. The strange, too-perfect timing of every attack. He’d seen all of it and chosen not to look. He should have copied her class the very first day. One touch. That’s all it would have taken to know.","\"It’s not your fault,\" Nathia said.","\"Yes it is. I should have been better.\"","\"How could you have known?\" She kept her eyes fixed on the path ahead, refusing to look at him. \"None of us saw it.\"","Night pressed in between the trees, thick and close. Rael didn’t ask about her life before the realm, and he offered nothing of his own. The less they spoke of the ordinary world, the easier it was to keep moving, to keep breathing. Beneath Nathia’s calm expression, something burned—quiet, constant, unforgiving. Because of Suzy, Luke was dead. The girl had smiled. She’d played helpless. And the whole time, she’d been arranging his death like a piece on a board.","\"She’s a sick bastard,\" Rael said, the words tasting like ash. \"All of this is just a game to her.\"","They hadn’t gone much farther when a low shape moved between the trunks ahead.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Dire Wolf","Level: 8","Tier: Feral","No pack. Exactly the kind of target Rael needed—souls, without the risk of another jump in his own level.","Nathia raised both hands. \"Water Shackles.\"","Coils of water erupted from the ground and locked around the wolf’s legs. It snarled and thrashed against them, but the bonds held just long enough. Rael formed a Fireball and drove it straight into the creature’s skull. Fur and skin blackened instantly. He didn’t wait—a second Fireball followed before the wolf could even recover its footing. The burned head tore loose from the body and rolled into the undergrowth.","[+4 souls]","Nathia stepped closer to the corpse, almost casual. \"That was easy.\"","\"Yeah.\"","Then the temperature dropped. Without warning. Without mercy.","A cold so sharp it felt like knives pressed flat against bare skin settled over the clearing. The air itself turned heavy, resistant, hard to drag into the lungs. Every hair on Rael’s arms rose at once. Nathia felt it in the same instant—her breath caught audibly, and she turned, slow and stiff, the same dread spreading across her face that he could feel gripping his own chest.","Rael looked back.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Frost Dire Wolf","Level: 50","Tier: Elite","The Frost Dire Wolf stood between the trees like a piece of the night itself, given shape and teeth. Its body was the size of a bus. Its pure white fur caught what little moonlight made it through the canopy and threw it back cold and sharp. Teeth jutted past its lips, long as knives. Its eyes glowed a deep, steady red, unblinking, fixed on him. Dark bloodstains marred the white coat in patches, and near the muzzle the fur was gone entirely, leaving raw, exposed skin.","The wolf opened its mouth and roared.","The sound hit like a physical blow, a wall of noise that rang in Rael’s ears and rattled something behind his ribs. It shoved him back a step even as he locked his legs and refused—refused—to give any more ground. Nathia went down hard, knees in the dirt, hands pressed flat against the earth like she was trying to hold the whole forest still.","\"How can we ever beat something like that?\" she whispered, and her voice cracked on the last word.","Rael could see the old wounds more clearly now—the missing fur, the scarred, raw skin around the mouth. He drew the goblin blade anyway. There was nowhere left to run, and some part of him had already accepted that. Sweat beaded cold on his forehead despite the freezing air biting at his skin. He waited for the charge. It had to come. It always came.","It never did.","The Frost Dire Wolf only stared at him. It ignored Nathia completely, as if she weren’t even there. After a long, unbearable moment, it turned and began walking to the left—then stopped, and looked back over its shoulder. The meaning was unmistakable, impossible to misread.","Follow.","Rael pulled Nathia to her feet. She was still shaking, her hand cold in his.","\"We have to follow it,\" he said.","\"Are you mad?\"","The wolf roared again—shorter this time, sharper, impatient. Nathia flinched hard but somehow stayed on her feet.","\"If it wanted us dead,\" Rael said, \"it would have finished it already.\"","They started walking. The great white shape moved ahead of them at an easy, unhurried pace, never letting the distance stretch too wide, never letting them forget it was there. Rael kept the blade in his hand and watched for any chance to break away, but the forest on either side felt watched, sealed shut, like something unseen was holding its breath along with them. Neither of them spoke. There was nothing to say that wouldn’t sound small against the silence.","After a time, the wolf stopped in a small clearing and began scraping at the ground with one massive paw. Dirt flew in dark clumps. It looked at Rael. Then at the disturbed earth. Then back at Rael again, waiting.","\"You want me to dig here?\" he asked, and his own voice sounded strange to him in the quiet.","The thought came uninvited and refused to leave: that the beast was telling him to dig his own grave. Nathia’s hand closed hard around his arm.","\"Don’t.\"","He shook his head and knelt anyway. The soil gave way soft beneath his fingers. It didn’t take long before he felt something solid—a long wooden shaft. He pulled it free and brushed the dirt from it with trembling hands. A spear. He’d seen this exact weapon before. He knew it.","\"Alex,\" he said quietly, barely more than breath. \"This is Alex’s spear.\"","Nathia stared at it, then at the wolf, understanding and horror dawning together on her face. Rael turned fully toward the massive white creature and made himself meet its red eyes. Something familiar lived in them now that he looked past the fear, something that made his chest ache.","\"Alex?\" he asked.","The wolf did not move.","Rael took a step closer, his voice barely above a whisper.","\"Are you Alex?\"","---"]},{"number":24,"title":"Chapter 24: Search","paragraphs":["\"Alex is that you?\" Rael asked this weird question knowing how can this be possible.","The Frost Dire Wolf nodded.","The motion was slow and deliberate, a single dip of that massive white head. Rael felt the ground tilt under him. Two explanations flashed through his mind, neither of them comforting. Either the Summoner could force a false story into a beast’s actions, or Alex had somehow become this creature. Before he could speak again the wolf’s body seized.","It began to shake.","The tremors started in the shoulders and raced down the spine. White fur rippled in violent waves. The red eyes rolled, then locked, then rolled again. A low, broken sound tore from its throat—half roar, half something closer to a human groan of pain. The great head swung from side to side as if trying to throw off an invisible weight. Claws gouged deep furrows in the frozen earth. Frost exploded outward in jagged sheets each time a paw slammed down.","\"Alex—Alex, listen to me.\" Rael kept his voice steady even though every instinct screamed to step back. \"How can I help you? How do I turn you back?\"","The wolf forced its jaws open. Only a thin, strangled roar escaped. The red eyes found Rael’s for a single clear second. In that second the message was plain: the Summoner’s will was crushing whatever remained of Alex. The wolf’s head jerked east, once, hard. Then the last of its resistance broke. It spun and bolted in the opposite direction, crashing through the trees with a sound like a falling landslide, leaving only swirling mist and the lingering cold.","Rael turned to Nathia. \"We go east.\"","\"Ok...\" Her voice was tight.","They ran.","The forest blurred past. Branches whipped at their arms and faces. Distant howls rose ahead of them, first one, then many, weaving together into a constant, low chorus. An image of a mountain began to take shape through the trees—dark stone rising higher with every stride, its upper slopes lost in cloud. The closer they came, the larger it grew, until it filled half the night sky. The howls thickened. Roars joined them. The whole mountain sounded alive with monsters.","They slowed.","Rushing in blind would get them killed. Rael raised a hand and they moved at a careful walk, eyes sweeping every shadow. A tree to their left shook hard enough to drop a rain of dead leaves. There was no wind. Rael turned. Nothing. Another branch trembled farther back, then went still. Something was traveling through the canopy, light and patient, waiting for an opening.","\"Be careful,\" he said quietly. \"Something’s wrong here.\"","Then every sound stopped.","The distant howls cut off as if a door had closed. The soft rustle of leaves vanished. Even the insects that should have been singing in the dark fell silent. Rael could hear only his own breathing and the faint thud of Nathia’s heart when she stepped close enough. The air pressed in, thick and watching.","A shape dropped from the trees directly in front of him.","He threw himself sideways. Claws the color of old iron tore through the space where his chest had been a heartbeat earlier.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Ironhide Gorilla","Level: 23","Tier: Savage","A second gorilla landed behind them with a heavy, wet sound. Rael and Nathia spun until their backs touched. The two beasts circled, white eyes empty and milky, jaws that looked forged from metal rather than bone. The lower fangs curved upward like shears. If either creature closed those jaws on an arm or leg, the limb would simply come away. Even their nails gleamed like hammered iron. The smell that rolled off them was the same thick, rotten sweetness Rael had noticed on every high-level beast since the beginning—the smell of things that should already be dead.","His mind raced. Suzy could not have unlimited numbers of these. The Frost Dire Wolf and these gorillas might be the last of her stronger pieces. Somehow she had forced Savage-tier creatures into the First Realm, something that should have been impossible for a new awakener. Every one of them had carried that corpse reek. He still did not understand how. The only way to learn was to copy her class, and for that he needed to get close enough to touch her.","The gorilla in front bounced once on its knuckles, then launched.","Nathia stepped into its path. \"Water Wall!\"","A thick sheet of water rose between them and the charging beast. The gorilla smashed through it without slowing. Rael yanked Nathia aside. Iron nails raked across her upper arm, opening three long, shallow cuts. She hissed but stayed on her feet.","Both gorillas were in front of them now. Rael pulled Nathia behind him and set his feet. He could feel her shaking against his back. The white eyes watched without hunger or anger—only the flat attention of things following orders. The rotten smell thickened until it coated the inside of his mouth.","He needed a plan. Standing and trading blows would get them both killed. The mountain loomed above, full of more howls waiting to start again. Somewhere on those slopes Lila was still alive—or already past saving. And somewhere in the dark the Summoner who wore Suzy’s face was watching.","Rael tightened his grip on the goblin blade and waited for the next charge.","---"]},{"number":25,"title":"Chapter 25: Ironhide Gorillas","paragraphs":["The two Ironhide Gorillas closed in under the shadow of the mountain. The air here was colder than the rest of the forest, thick with the same rotten sweetness that clung to every high-level beast Suzy had summoned. Mist curled low around the roots. Distant howls still echoed from the higher slopes, but in this small clearing the only sounds were the slow scrape of iron nails on stone and Nathia’s uneven breathing behind Rael’s back.","She was shaking. She squeezed her eyes shut, trying not to see them, but tears still slipped down her cheeks. Rael felt every tremor. The shape of the gorillas—thick arms, heavy shoulders, the way they moved on their knuckles—dragged the memory of the ape that had killed Luke straight to the surface. He kept his body between her and the white-eyed creatures and spoke without turning.","\"Nathia. I need your help. I can’t do this without you.\"","For a moment she said nothing. Then the warmth of his back seemed to reach her. She opened her eyes. The fear was still there, but she wiped her face with the back of one hand and answered in a thin, steady voice.","\"Okay... buy me a few minutes.\"","Mana began to gather around her wand in visible blue threads. Rael nodded once. He understood. She had a plan. His only job was to keep her alive until it was ready.","He had brought Lila’s bow with him; leaving it behind had never been an option. He swung it off his shoulder, nocked an arrow, and drew the string fully. It was his first time using a bow, yet the Archer class guided his hands as if he had done it a hundred times.","\"Piercing Arrow.\"","The shaft left the string with a sharp hiss and a thin white trail. The nearest gorilla twisted aside at the last instant; the arrow buried itself in a tree behind it. The second beast charged.","It was too close for another shot. Rael dropped the bow and swept his free hand across the ground.","\"Ignite.\"","Flames erupted under the gorilla’s lead foot. The creature bellowed and leapt back, iron nails scoring deep lines in the dirt. The calm expression never left Rael’s face, but he knew the truth: these skills alone would not be enough.","The situation was desperate. He could not move from his place or Nathia would be exposed, and none of his current skills were strong enough to punch through that iron-hard hide. His mind raced, pulling every scrap of information he had. An idea formed—uncertain, but better than standing still.","He nocked another arrow, formed a small Fireball against the tip, and released. The combined force launched the shaft far harder than before. A thick black trail of smoke followed it through the dark. The arrow punched into the first gorilla’s neck. Fire clung to the wound and kept burning even after the shaft stopped moving. The beast screamed, a raw, metallic sound, and tore the arrow free, but the flames refused to die.","He could combine skills. It was the first time he had tried it, and the discovery opened a dozen new possibilities even as the fight continued.","The second gorilla ripped a boulder from the ground and raised it overhead. Rael put another fire-tipped arrow into its forearm. The creature roared and hurled the rock anyway. He could have dodged. He stayed where he was. Moving would leave Nathia open.","\"Ignite. Ignite. Ignite.\"","Flames burst across the surface of the flying stone, but the mass was too great. The trajectory barely shifted. The boulder smashed into Rael’s right shoulder and arm. Something inside the joint tore free with a wet pop. Pain exploded white-hot up his neck. He staggered, hissed through his teeth, and forced himself not to drop.","He could no longer feel his right hand. With no other options left he turned to the only cover he could still make. His left hand hurled Fireball after Fireball into the dirt at their feet. Thick black smoke boiled upward, swallowing the clearing in seconds. The gorillas roared, blind and furious, swinging at empty air.","Inside the smoke Nathia’s eyes snapped open. Blue light shone in them.","\"Water Burst.\"","The spell left her hands with a force that cracked the air. A concentrated spear of water slammed into the first gorilla’s chest and hurled the heavy body backward through the trees as if it weighed nothing. Nathia collapsed to one knee, gasping. The last of her mana was gone.","The remaining gorilla saw her drop and charged straight through the smoke.","Rael stepped into its path. He forced his dislocated right arm up at the last possible moment. Iron jaws closed around his forearm with a sound like metal shearing bone. The pain was absolute. He screamed, but the scream turned into a single word.","\"Fireball.\"","Flame erupted inside the gorilla’s mouth. Fire roared out through its eyes, its nostrils, the gaps between its iron teeth. The beast’s head became a lantern of pure heat for one terrible second. Then the body locked and toppled forward, jaws still locked on Rael’s ruined arm.","[You killed an Ironhide Gorilla.]","[+78 souls]","Silence returned to the clearing, broken only by the soft crackle of dying flames and Nathia’s ragged breathing. The mountain loomed above them, still full of distant howls, and somewhere higher up the slopes Lila was still waiting—or already gone.","Before he could turn to Nathia, something heavy struck the back of his head. The world tilted. Darkness rushed in.","Nathia’s voice followed him down.","\"Rael—!\"","---"]}]);
